Note that the convexity of $P _ { \mu }$ is equivalent to monotonicity of the quotients $k _ { i } / n _ { i }$. Shatz now defines the partial ordering by:
\[
\lambda \geqslant \mu \quad \text { if } \quad P _ { \lambda } \quad \text { is above } P _ { \mu } .
\]

If we consider $P _ { \mu }$ as the graph of a concave function $p _ { \mu }$, then $p _ { \mu }$ is defined at the integers by
\[
p _ { \mu } ( i ) = \sum _ { j \leqslant i } \mu _ { j }
\]
and interpolates linearly between integers. Here the $\mu _ { j }$ are the components of our $n$-vector $\mu$. Hence, for our vector notation, (7.6) translates into the following partial ordering:
\[
\lambda \geqslant \mu \quad \text { if } \quad \sum _ { j \leqslant i } \lambda _ { j } \geqslant \sum _ { j \leqslant i } \mu _ { j } , \quad j = 1 , \ldots , n - 1 .
\]
Note that $\Sigma \lambda _ { j } = \Sigma \mu _ { i } = k$ is fixed. This partial ordering on vectors in $R ^ { n }$ is well known in various contexts (see Marshall \& Olkin 1979) and we shall discuss its Lie group significance in § 12. For the present we return to the result of Shatz, which now takes the form
\[
\mathscr { C } _ { \mu } \subset \bigcup _ { \lambda \geqslant \mu } \mathscr { C } _ { \lambda } .
\]
In the next section we shall give a differential-geometric proof of (7.8) that is more in the spirit of this paper.

It is clear that this partial ordering on types satisfies condition (1.17). We shall check condition (1.18) later (see (7.16)). We can thus use the stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ by the $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ to describe the equivariant cohomology of $\mathscr { C }$ in terms of that of the $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$. It remains to show that this stratification is 'perfect' in the sense of §1.

Let $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ denote the space of all $C ^ { \infty }$ filtrations of $E$ of type $\mu$. Thus points $f _ { \mu } \in \mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ are sections of the fibre bundle over $M$ with fibre the manifold $F _ { \mu } = G L ( n , C ) / B _ { \mu }$ where $B _ { \mu }$ is the parabolic subgroup preserving a fixed (partial) flag of subspaces of $C ^ { n }$ of dimensions $n _ { 1 } , n _ { 1 } + n _ { 2 } , \ldots$. The sequence of Chern classes $k _ { i }$ corresponds to picking a definite component of the space of all sections. Since the filtration (7.1) is canonical we have a map (the continuity of which will be established in §§ 14 and 15)
\[
\mathscr { C } _ { \mu } \rightarrow \mathscr { F } _ { \mu } .
\]

If we fix a base-point of $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ corresponding to a definite $C ^ { \infty }$ filtration $E _ { \mu }$ of $E$, the fibre of (7.9) over this point is the subspace $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } \subset \mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ of complex structures compatible with the given filtration. If $\operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right)$ is the group of $C ^ { \infty }$ automorphisms of $E$ preserving the filtration then $\operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right)$ acts on $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } , \mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ is the homogeneous space $\operatorname { Aut } ( E ) / \operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right)$ and $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ can be identified with the associated bundle. Hence for the purposes of equivariant cohomology the pairs
\[
\left( \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) , \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } \right) \quad \text { and } \quad \left( \operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right) , \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } \right)
\]
are equivalent as explained in § 13.
Next let us choose splittings of the filtration $E _ { \mu }$ so that we get a direct sum decomposition $E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$ of $E$
\[
E = D _ { 1 } \oplus D _ { 2 } \oplus \ldots \oplus D _ { r }
\]
with
\[
E _ { i } = D _ { 1 } \oplus \ldots \oplus D _ { i } .
\]
and let $\operatorname { Aut } E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } , \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$ be the automorphisms and complex structures (in $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu }$ ) compatible with this decomposition. Then we have
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } \right) & \cong \prod _ { l = 1 } ^ { r } \operatorname { Aut } \left( D _ { i } \right) \\
\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } & \cong \prod _ { l = 1 } ^ { r } \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \left( D _ { i } \right)
\end{aligned}
\]
On the other hand the natural homomorphism
\[
\operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right) \rightarrow \operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } \right)
\]
is a homotopy equivalence, and the fibration
\[
\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } \rightarrow \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }
\]
has a vector space as fibre and is compatible with the group actions. It follows that, for purposes of equivariant cohomology, the pairs
\[
\left( \operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right) , \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } \right) \quad \text { and } \quad \left( \operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } \right) , \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } \right)
\]
are equivalent. Together with (7.11) this finally yields for rational cohomology
Proposition 7.12. The equivariant cohomology of the stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu } ( E )$ is isomorphic to the tensor product of the equivariant cohomology of the semi-stable strata for the quotients $D _ { i }$.
Here of course the equivariant cohomology is always taken with respect to the automorphism group of the appropriate bundle.

We also need to look at the equivariant cohomology of the conormal bundle $N _ { \mu }$ to $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ in $\mathscr { C }$. By this we mean of course the appropriate relative cohomology or the cohomology of the Thom space of $N _ { \mu }$. Exactly the same reduction as above shows that we can replace the triple (Aut $( E )$, $\left. \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } , N _ { \mu } \right)$ by the triple (Aut $\left( E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } \right) , \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } , N _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$ ) where $N _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$ is the restriction of $N _ { \mu }$ to $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$. Now from (7.11) we see that $\operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } ^ { 0 } \right)$ contains the $r$-dimensional torus $T ^ { r }$, which acts trivially on $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$. To show that our stratification is perfect it remains to show, using (1.9) and (13.4), that the representation of $T ^ { r }$ on the fibre of $N _ { \mu }$ is primitive. Now at a point of $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { 0 }$ our bundle $E$ is a holomorphic direct sum of the $D _ { i }$ and so the bundle $\operatorname { End } ^ { \prime } E$ of endomorphisms preserving the filtration is the direct sum of $\operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { i } , D _ { j } \right)$ for $i \geqslant j$. Hence
\[
\operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E \cong \underset { i < j } { \oplus } \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { i } , D _ { j } \right) .
\]

Now on $\operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { i } , D _ { j } \right)$ the element $\left( t _ { 1 } , \ldots , t _ { r } \right) \in T ^ { r }$ acts by $t _ { i } ^ { - 1 } t _ { j }$ and so it acts by the same character on $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { i } , D _ { j } \right) \right)$. Since the fibre of $N$ is $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E \right)$ it follows from (7.13) that the representation of $T ^ { r }$ on $N$ is indeed primitive. Thus we have proved

Theorem 7.14. The stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ by the $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ is equivariantly perfect so that for the equivariant Poincaré series we have
\[
P _ { t } ( \mathscr { C } ) = \sum _ { \mu } t ^ { 2 d \mu } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } \right)
\]
where $d _ { \mu }$ is the complex codimension of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$.
The dimension $d _ { \mu }$ can be calculated by Riemann-Roch, since $H ^ { 0 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E \right) = 0$, and we find (as in (5.10))
\[
d _ { \mu } = \sum _ { \mu _ { i } > \mu _ { j } } \left\{ \mu _ { i } - \mu _ { j } + ( g - 1 ) \right\} .
\]
Alternatively, in terms of the sequence $\left( n _ { 1 } , k _ { 1 } \right) , \ldots , \left( n _ { r } , k _ { r } \right)$,
\[
d _ { \mu } = \sum _ { i > j } \left\{ \left( n _ { i } k _ { j } - n _ { j } k _ { i } \right) + n _ { i } n _ { j } ( g - 1 ) \right\} .
\]
In particular this shows that our stratification does indeed satisfy the finiteness condition (1.18).
The first term in the series of (7.14) arises from the semi-stable bundles. All the remainder can be calculated inductively by (7.12). Hence knowledge about $P _ { t } ( \mathscr { C } )$ (from §1) leads to inductive formulae for $P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right)$.

Since the stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ is perfect over the integers we can also deduce results about torsion. First we should note that the equivariant cohomology of $\mathscr { C }$, namely the cohomology of $B$ Aut $( E )$, has no torsion. This follows from the identification with $B \mathscr { G }$, to be explained in § 8, together with the results of §2. It follows therefore that all strata $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ have no torsion (equivariantly). In particular therefore
\[
\text { the semi-stable stratum } \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \text { has no torsion in its equivariant cohomology. }
\]

In the coprime case $( n , k ) = 1$ we have $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } = \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ and $\operatorname { Aut } ( E )$ acts on $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ with the constant scalars as the only isotropy group (Narasimhan \& Seshadri 1965). From this we can derive results for the ordinary cohomology of the moduli space $N ( n , k ) = \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } / \operatorname { Aut } ( E )$. Thus we get a formula for its Poincaré polynomial and we shall also see that it has no torsion. This will be treated in detail in § 9.

\section*{8. Relation with Yang-Mills}

In the previous section we have given a purely complex analytic approach to the moduli space of stable bundles. We want now to look at the same problem from the unitary or differentialgeometric point of view. The connecting link is the key result of Narasimhan \& Seshadri (1965) identifying stable bundles as those that arise from irreducible unitary representations. Translated into the notation we have introduced in § 6 their result can be formulated as follows.
(8.1) A holomorphic vector bundle of rank $n$ is stable if and only if it arises from an irreducible representation $\rho : \Gamma _ { R } \rightarrow U ( n )$. Moreover isomorphic bundles correspond to equivalent representations.

Remarks. 1. Actually the description given by Narasimhan \& Seshadri (1965) is slightly different from (8.1) though equivalent to it. They puncture $M$ at one point $p$ and consider coverings of $M$ with ramification of order $n$ at $p$. This leads to a purely holomorphic description whereas our version, with connections, is a differential-geometric version.
2. Donaldson (1983) has recently given a new proof of (8.1) in the spirit of this paper.

To understand the geometric significance of (8.1) we recall first, as explained in §5, that a unitary connection $A$ for a vector bundle $E$ over our Riemann surface $M$ defines a holomorphic structure simply by taking the $( 0,1 )$-component $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime }$ of the covariant derivative $\mathrm { d } _ { A }$. This gives a map $\mathscr { A } \rightarrow \mathscr { C }$, which is in fact an affine-linear isomorphism. Locally this corresponds to the isomorphism
\[
\Omega ^ { 1 } ( \mathfrak { u } ( n ) ) \cong \Omega ^ { 0,1 } ( \mathfrak { g } \mathfrak { l } ( n , C ) )
\]
for the Lie algebra valued 1-forms. Note that $\mathscr { C }$ is defined independently of any metric on the bundle, whereas $\mathscr { A }$ and hence the isomorphism $\mathscr { A } \rightarrow \mathscr { C }$ depend on such a choice. The connection $A$ associated in this way to the holomorphic structure will be called the metric connection. Since any two metrics differ by a complex gauge transformation, i.e. an element of Aut $( E )$, it will be immaterial which metric we pick. The group $\operatorname { Aut } ( E )$ may now be viewed as the complexification $\mathscr { C } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ of the group of unitary gauge transformations $\mathscr { G }$ of $E$.

Now let $\mathscr { N } \subset \mathscr { A }$ denote the set of connections giving the minimum for the Yang-Mills functional. As we have shown in §6 these are precisely $\mathscr { G }$-equivalent to those given by representations $\rho : \Gamma _ { R } \rightarrow U ( n )$ with $\rho ( R )$ central. Let $\mathcal { N } _ { \mathrm { s } } \subset \mathcal { N }$ be those given by irreducible representations. Then (8.1) can be reformulated as follows:
\[
\text { Under the identification of } \mathscr { A } \text { with } \mathscr { C } \text {, we have } \mathcal { N } _ { \mathrm { s } } \subset \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \text { and the induced map of quotient spaces }
\]
\[
\mathcal { N } _ { \mathrm { s } } / \mathscr { G } \rightarrow \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } / \mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }
\]
is a homeomorphism.
The proof of Narasimhan \& Seshadri (1965) is essentially of (8.2). It is easy to prove the inclusion $\mathcal { N } _ { \mathrm { s } } \subset \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ and infinitesimal arguments show that the map of quotients (which are manifolds) is a local diffeomorphism. The hardest part of the proof is the surjectivity and this requires compactifying both sides and a consequent induction on $n$. The real explanation for (8.2) is probably to be found in the moment map ideas indicated at the end of §10 (see also remark 2 above).

Note that the quotient space $\mathscr { G } / \mathscr { G }$ may be identified with the space of hermitian metrics on $E$. Since this is a convex set in a linear space it is contractible and so $\mathscr { G }$ and $\mathscr { G }$ c have the same homotopy type. Hence equivariant cohomology is the same for the two groups.

Since direct sums of stable bundles with the same 'slope' are semi-stable it follows that $\mathscr { N } \subset \mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$ : note also that $\mathcal { N } = \mathcal { N } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ in the coprime case. More generally now let us transport the stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ by the $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ defined in § 7, to give a stratification of $\mathscr { A }$ by strata $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$. Let $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$ denote the Yang-Mills connections whose curvature is of type $\mu$. Such connections are direct sums of connections of the form $\mathscr { N } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ for smaller ranks. This shows that $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu } \subset \mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$.

Our first aim in this section is to show how to characterize the $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ strata by properties of the curvature. In particular we shall eventually show that the $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ are the 'Morse strata' of the Yang-Mills functional. In fact we shall prove that this holds for a much wider class of functionals than Yang-Mills. These functionals are obtained as follows. Let $\phi$ be any smooth function on the Lie algebra $\mathfrak { g }$ of $G$ that is invariant under the adjoint action and is convex. For example when $G = U ( n )$, so that $x \in \mathfrak { g }$ is a skew-hermitian matrix with eigenvalues $\mathrm { i } \lambda _ { 1 } , \ldots , \mathrm { i } \lambda _ { n }$, we can take for $\phi$ any of the following
\[
\left. \begin{array} { l } 
\sum \lambda _ { j } ^ { 2 k } , \\
\exp \left( k \lambda _ { j } \right) ,
\end{array} \right\} \quad k = 1,2 , \ldots
\]

These can be written directly in terms of $x$, without resort to the eigenvalues, and are clearly smooth invariant functions. Why they are convex will be explained in § 12 when we discuss this notion more systematically. Given such a $\phi$ we now define a function $\Phi$ on the space of $G$ -connections over $M$ in the obvious way:
\[
\Phi ( A ) = \int _ { M } \phi ( * F ( A ) ) .
\]
This reduces to the Yang-Mills functional when $\phi ( x ) =$ trace $x ^ { * } x$ (corresponding to $k = 1$ in (i) above). Since $\phi$ is invariant under the adjoint action, $\Phi$ is gauge-invariant. Taking $A _ { t } = A + t \eta$ and computing as in §4 we see that
\[
\Phi \left( A _ { t } \right) = \Phi ( A ) + t \int \left\langle \phi ^ { \prime } ( * F ( A ) ) , * \mathrm {~d} _ { A } \eta \right\rangle \bmod t ^ { 2 }
\]
where $\phi ^ { \prime } : \mathrm { g } \rightarrow \mathrm { g }$ is the derivative of $\phi$, i.e.
\[
\phi ( x + t y ) = \phi ( x ) + t \left\langle \phi ^ { \prime } ( x ) , y \right\rangle \bmod t ^ { 2 } .
\]
From (8.4) we see that the gradient of $\Phi$, relative to the metric on the space of connections, is
\[
\operatorname { grad } \Phi = - * \mathrm {~d} _ { A } \phi ^ { \prime } ( * F ( A ) ) .
\]
This reduces to the formulae of §4 for the Yang-Mills functional when $\phi ( x ) = \operatorname { trace } x ^ { * } x$ so that $\frac { 1 } { 2 } \phi ^ { \prime }$ is the identity map $\mathrm { g } \rightarrow \mathrm { g }$. In general $\phi ^ { \prime }$ is an equivariant map (relative to the adjoint action of $G$ ) and so for any section $s$ of $\operatorname { ad } ( P )$ the covariant derivative of $\phi ^ { \prime } ( s )$ can be obtained from that of $s$ by the composition rule
\[
\mathrm { d } _ { A } \phi ^ { \prime } ( s ) = \phi ^ { \prime \prime } ( s ) \circ \mathrm { d } _ { A } s .
\]
This is most easily understood by viewing $s$ as an equivariant function $P \rightarrow \mathrm {~g}$ and noting that $\mathrm { d } _ { A } s$ is just the horizontal part (relative to the connection $A$ ) of the ordinary differential $\mathrm { d } s$.

From (8.5) and (8.6) we deduce at once
(8.7) a critical connection for the Yang-Mills functional $L$ is also critical for $\Phi$, and the converse holds if $\phi$ is strictly convex.

By a strictly convex function we mean as usual a function for which the second derivative $\phi ^ { \prime \prime }$ is everywhere positive definite, so that the linear transformation $\phi ^ { \prime \prime } ( s )$ in (8.6) is invertible. The quadratic function trace $x ^ { * } x$ used for the Yang-Mills function is clearly strictly convex and any (weakly) convex $\phi$ becomes strongly convex if we add a positive multiple of trace $x ^ { * } x$. We see therefore that there are many functionals $\Phi$ that have precisely the same set of critical points as the Yang-Mills functional $L$.

So far we have not really used the convexity of $\phi$, only the non-degeneracy of the second derivative $\phi ^ { \prime \prime }$. Thus $- \phi$ would have had the same properties. The significance of convexity is that the Hessian of $\Phi$ always has a finite Morse index. To see this we compute as in §4 and find that the Hessian $H$ corresponds to the self-adjoint differential operator
\[
\Phi _ { A } = \phi ^ { \prime \prime } ( * F ( A ) ) \mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { * } \mathrm {~d} _ { A } + \text { lower order. }
\]
Since $\Phi$ is gauge invariant we can, as in §4, restrict ourselves to $\operatorname { ad } ( P )$-valued 1-forms $\eta$ with $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { * } \eta = 0$, so that in (8.8) $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { * } \mathrm {~d} _ { A }$ can be replaced by the covariant Laplacian
\[
\Delta _ { A } = \mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { * } \mathrm {~d} _ { A } + \mathrm { d } _ { A } \mathrm {~d} _ { A } ^ { * } .
\]

Then $\Phi _ { A }$ becomes a second-order elliptic differential operator and the (strong) convexity of $\phi$ asserts that the leading-order terms are positive definite. This is enough to make the spectrum discrete and bounded below so that there are only finitely many negative eigenvalues, showing that $H _ { \Phi }$ has finite Morse index.

Equations (8.5) and (8.6), together with the strong convexity of $\phi$, imply that
\[
( \operatorname { grad } \Phi , \operatorname { grad } L ) \geqslant 0
\]
with equality only if $A$ is a critical connection. This means that $\Phi$ is strictly decreasing along the paths of steepest descent for $L$, i.e. the trajectories of $- \operatorname { grad } L$. In finite dimensions this would imply that at the common critical points the Morse indices of $L$ and $\Phi$ coincide. In our situation this can be seen directly as follows. Expanding (8.9) at a critical connection $A$, and discarding higher-order terms, we deduce
\[
\left( H _ { \Phi } \eta , H _ { L } \eta \right) \geqslant 0
\]
with equality only if $\eta$ is in the null-space of $H _ { L }$ (which coincides with that of $H _ { \Phi }$ ). By restricting $\eta$ to the negative space $V$ of $H _ { L }$ we reduce (8.10) to a finite-dimensional inequality, which easily implies that $H _ { \Phi }$ is negative definite on $V$ (e.g. diagonalize $H _ { L }$ on $V$ ). Thus the Morse index of $\Phi$ is at least equal to that of $L$. Reversing the roles of $L$ and $\Phi$ we get therefore
(8.11) the Morse indices of $\Phi$ and $L$ all coincide.

To sum up we see that any one of our functionals $\Phi$, defined by a strongly convex invariant function $\phi$ on g , has exactly the same critical point structure as the Yang-Mills functional $L$. Our next aim is to show that all such functionals lead in fact to the same Morse strata and that these strata coincide with the complex strata introduced in §7.

We now return to the identification of the space $\mathscr { A }$ of unitary connections with the space $\mathscr { C }$ of complex structures, explained at the beginning of this section, together with the actions of the groups $\mathscr { G }$ and $\mathscr { G }$ c of unitary and complex automorphisms. The tangent space to the $\mathscr { G }$-orbit through $A$ consists of vectors $\mathrm { d } _ { A } \alpha$ with $\alpha \in \Omega ^ { 0 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$ while for the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-orbit it consists of $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime } \beta$ with $\beta \in \Omega ^ { 0 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } \left( P ^ { \mathrm { c } } \right) \right)$. Since we are identifying $\Omega ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$ with $\Omega ^ { 0,1 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } \left( P ^ { \mathrm { c } } \right) \right)$ on which $* = \mathrm { i }$ we can say that the tangent space to the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-orbit at $A$ consists of vectors
\[
\mathrm { d } _ { A } \alpha _ { 1 } + * \mathrm {~d} _ { A } \alpha _ { 2 } \quad \text { with } \quad \alpha _ { 1 } , \alpha _ { 2 } \in \Omega ^ { 0 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) ) .
\]
In particular then formula (8.5) for $\operatorname { grad } \Phi$ shows that
\[
\operatorname { grad } \Phi \text { is tangential to the } \mathscr { G } _ { - } \text {orbits. }
\]
In other words the 'gradient flow' of $\Phi$ preserves the $\mathscr { G }$ c-orbits and hence also the strata $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ of §7 since these are unions of orbits. Since the stratum $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ contains a unique component $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$ of the critical set of $\Phi$ it is then reasonable to expect $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ to be just the Morse stratum or stable manifold of $\Phi$ associated with $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$. For this to be true it is of course necessary that $\Phi$ on $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ should take its minimum on $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$. Now for any $A \in \mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$ the conjugacy class of $* F ( A )$ is constant and is represented by the skew-hermitian diagonal matrix $\Lambda _ { \mu }$ with entries $- 2 \pi \mathrm { i } \mu _ { j } ( j = 1 , \ldots , n )$. Since the volume of $M$ is normalized to be unity it follows that $\Phi ( A )$ takes the constant value $\phi \left( \Lambda _ { \mu } \right)$ and we shall write this simply as $\phi ( \mu )$. For example, for the Yang-Mills functional, we have
\[
\phi ( \mu ) = \| \mu \| ^ { 2 } = \Sigma \mu _ { i } ^ { 2 } .
\]
Thus we might expect the following to hold.

Proposition 8.13. For any $A \in \mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$, and for any convex invariant function $\phi$ on $\mathfrak { u } ( n )$, we have $\Phi ( A ) \geqslant \phi ( \mu )$.

We shall begin by proving (8.13) in the simple case when
\[
\mu _ { 1 } = \mu _ { 2 } = \ldots = \mu _ { r } > \mu _ { r + 1 } = \ldots = \mu _ { n }
\]
so that the canonical filtration of the bundle $E$ has just two steps. This means that, for the holomorphic structure defined by $A \in \mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$, we have an exact sequence of vector bundles
\[
0 \rightarrow D _ { 1 } \rightarrow E \rightarrow D _ { 2 } \rightarrow 0 ,
\]
where $D _ { j }$ has rank $m _ { j }$, Chern class $k _ { j } ( j = 1,2 )$ so that $\mu _ { 1 } = k _ { 1 } / m _ { 1 }$ and $\mu _ { n } = k _ { 2 } / m _ { 2 }$. For convenience we shall use the notation $\mu ^ { j } = k _ { j } / m _ { j } ( j = 1,2 )$. The curvature $F ( A )$ can then be written in the form
\[
F ( A ) = \left[ \begin{array} { c c } 
F _ { 1 } - \eta \wedge \eta ^ { * } & \mathrm {~d} \eta \\
- \mathrm { d } \eta ^ { * } & F _ { 2 } - \eta ^ { * } \wedge \eta
\end{array} \right] ,
\]
where $F _ { j }$ is the curvature of the metric connection of $D _ { j } , \eta \in \Omega ^ { 0,1 } \left( M , \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { 2 } , D _ { 1 } \right) \right) , \eta ^ { * }$ is its transposed conjugate and $\mathrm { d } \eta$ is the covariant differential. Now let $f _ { j } , \alpha _ { j }$ be scalar $m _ { j } \times m _ { j }$ matrices such that
\[
\begin{gathered}
\operatorname { trace } f _ { j } = \operatorname { trace } * F _ { j } \\
\operatorname { trace } \alpha _ { 1 } = \operatorname { trace } * \left( \eta \wedge \eta ^ { * } \right) = - \operatorname { trace } * \left( \eta ^ { * } \wedge \eta \right) = - \operatorname { trace } \alpha _ { 2 } .
\end{gathered}
\]
Then some elementary inequalities concerning convex invariant functions $\phi$ (which will be treated in §12) show that
\[
\phi ( * F ( A ) ) \geqslant \phi \left[ \begin{array} { c c } 
f _ { 1 } - \alpha _ { 1 } & 0 \\
0 & f _ { 2 } - \alpha _ { 2 }
\end{array} \right] .
\]
The convexity of $\phi$, together with the fact that $M$ has normalized volume, then implies that
\[
\Phi ( A ) = \int _ { M } \phi ( * F ( A ) ) \geqslant \phi \int _ { M } \left[ \begin{array} { c c } 
f _ { 1 } - \alpha _ { 1 } & 0 \\
0 & f _ { 2 } - \alpha _ { 2 }
\end{array} \right] .
\]
But the Chern class $k _ { j }$ of $D _ { j }$ is given by
\[
k _ { j } = \frac { \mathrm { i } } { 2 \pi } \int _ { M } \operatorname { trace } f _ { j } .
\]
Since $f _ { j }$ is a scalar matrix this means that $\int _ { M } f _ { j }$ is a scalar matrix whose diagonal entries are $- 2 \pi \mathrm { i } k _ { j } / m _ { j } = - 2 \pi \mathrm { i } \mu ^ { j }$. Also from (8.15) (since $\eta \in \Omega ^ { 0,1 }$ ) it follows that -i trace $\alpha _ { 1 }$ is non-negative and so
\[
\int _ { M } \alpha _ { 1 } = 2 \pi \mathrm { i } a _ { 1 } ,
\]
where $a _ { 1 }$ is a non-negative scalar $m _ { 1 } \times m _ { 1 }$ matrix. Then
\[
\int _ { M } \alpha _ { 2 } = 2 \pi \mathrm { i } a _ { 2 } ,
\]
where $a _ { 2 }$ is the non-positive scalar $m _ { 2 } \times m _ { 2 }$ matrix such that trace $a _ { 2 } = - \operatorname { trace } a _ { 1 }$. Hence we have
\[
\int _ { M } \left[ \begin{array} { c c } 
f _ { 1 } - \alpha _ { 1 } & 0 \\
0 & f _ { 2 } - \alpha _ { 2 }
\end{array} \right] = - 2 \pi \mathrm { i } [ \mu + a ]
\]
where [ ] denotes the diagonal matrix defined by a vector, so that [ $a$ ] denotes the matrix $\left[ \begin{array} { c c } a _ { 1 } & 0 \\ 0 & a _ { 2 } \end{array} \right]$. From (8.16) and the convention we have adopted for defining $\phi$ on $n$-vectors (absorbing the factor $- 2 \pi \mathrm { i }$ ) we then obtain
\[
\Phi ( A ) \geqslant \phi ( \mu + a ) .
\]
But since $a _ { 1 } \geqslant 0 , a _ { 2 } \leqslant 0$ with trace $a _ { 1 } = - \operatorname { trace } a _ { 2 }$ it follows easily that $\mu + a \geqslant \mu$ with respect to the partial ordering defined in (7.7). As will be explained in § 12 this implies, and in fact is equivalent to,
\[
\phi ( \mu + a ) \geqslant \phi ( \mu )
\]
for all convex invariant $\phi$. This completes the proof of proposition 8.13 for the two-step case. As can be seen the essence of the proof is the basic principle that 'curvature decreases in holomorphic sub-bundles and increases in quotients' (Griffiths \& Harris 1978, p. 79). The general case of (8.13) proceeds in the same manner and we simply have to keep track of the notation. The details are as follows.

We start with a holomorphic bundle $E$ with its canonical filtration of type $\mu$ :
\[
0 = E _ { 0 } \subset E _ { 1 } \subset \ldots \subset E _ { r } = E
\]
where the quotients $D _ { j } = E _ { j } / E _ { j - 1 }$ have normalized Chern classes $\mu ^ { j }$ with
\[
\mu ^ { 1 } > \mu ^ { 2 } > \ldots > \mu ^ { r } .
\]
The curvature $F ( A )$ can then be expressed in a block form generalizing (8.14). For every $j < k$ we have an element
\[
\eta _ { j k } \in \Omega ^ { 0,1 } \left( M , \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { k } , D _ { j } \right) \right)
\]
so that $\mathrm { d } \eta _ { j k }$ appears in the $( j , k )$-block. The $\eta _ { j k }$ are the components of the element
\[
\eta _ { k } \in \Omega ^ { 0,1 } \left( M , \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { k } , E _ { k - 1 } \right) \right)
\]
related to the exact sequence
\[
0 \rightarrow E _ { k - 1 } \rightarrow E _ { k } \rightarrow D _ { k } \rightarrow 0 .
\]
Now define scalar non-negative $m _ { j } \times m _ { j }$ matrices $a _ { j k }$ for $j < k$ by
\[
\operatorname { trace } a _ { j k } = \frac { 1 } { 2 \pi \mathrm { i } } \int _ { M } \operatorname { trace } \left( \eta _ { j k } \wedge \eta _ { j k } ^ { * } \right) \geqslant 0 ,
\]
and define $a _ { k k }$ by
\[
\operatorname { trace } a _ { k k } = \frac { 1 } { 2 \pi \mathrm { i } } \int \operatorname { trace } \eta _ { k } ^ { * } \wedge \eta _ { k } \leqslant 0 ,
\]
so that $\sum _ { j \leqslant k }$ trace $a _ { j k } = 0$. Then the convexity of $\phi$ leads to the inequality
\[
\Phi ( A ) \geqslant \phi [ \mu + a ] ,
\]
where $a$ stands for the vector (or diagonal matrix) whose $j$ th block $a ^ { j }$ is the scalar (matrix)
\[
a ^ { j } = \sum _ { k \geqslant j } a _ { j k } .
\]
Equivalently the vector $a$ can be written as a sum
\[
a = \sum b _ { k }
\]
where $b _ { k }$ is the vector corresponding to the diagonal matrix whose $j$ th block is $a _ { j k }$ for $j \leqslant k$ (and zero for $j > k$ ). The fact that
\[
\text { trace } a _ { j k } \geqslant 0 \quad \text { for } \quad j < k \quad \text { and } \quad \sum _ { j \leqslant k } \text { trace } a _ { j k } = 0
\]
implies that $b _ { k } \geqslant 0$ relative to the partial ordering (7.7). Hence $a = \sum b _ { k } \geqslant 0$ and so $\mu + a \geqslant \mu$. As before this then implies that $\phi ( \mu + a ) \geqslant \phi ( \mu )$ and so completes the general proof of proposition 8.13.

It will be noted that we have nowhere used the maximal nature of the canonical filtration, i.e. the fact that the quotients $D _ { j }$ are semi-stable. Once we use this we shall be able to strengthen (8.13). For this we shall need to use the Narasimhan-Seshadri result (8.1).

If, for any holomorphic vector bundle $E$ over $M$, and any convex invariant $\phi$ we define
\[
\Phi ( E ) = \inf \Phi ( A ) ,
\]
where $A$ runs over all metric connections on $E$, then (8.1), together with proposition 8.13, implies that for stable bundles $E$ we have $\Phi ( E ) = \phi ( \mu )$. We shall now extend this to all bundles. First suppose we have a holomorphic exact sequence
\[
0 \rightarrow D _ { 1 } \rightarrow E \rightarrow D _ { 2 } \rightarrow 0 .
\]
Then a metric on $E$ gives rise to a connection whose curvature is given by (8.14). The element $\eta \in \Omega ^ { 0,1 } \left( M , \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { 2 } , D _ { 1 } \right) \right)$ defines a cohomology class $[ \eta ] \in H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { 2 } , D _ { 1 } \right) \right)$, which classifies the extension. Replacing $\eta$ by $t \eta$ with $t$ a non-zero constant alters the extension class but does not alter the isomorphism class of $E$, since the new extension is isomorphic to the original by a diagram of the form
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/e9a39117-9581-4f5f-a81d-70aed9c3fba4-54.jpg?height=163&width=521&top_left_y=1350&top_left_x=649)
Hence replacing $\eta$ by $t \eta$ and then letting $t \rightarrow 0$ shows that
\[
\Phi ( E ) \leqslant \Phi \left( D _ { 1 } \oplus D _ { 2 } \right) .
\]
Similarly if $E$ has a filtration of arbitrary length with quotients $D _ { j }$ we have
\[
\Phi ( E ) \leqslant \Phi \left( \oplus D _ { j } \right) .
\]
Now an elementary argument (see Seshadri 1967) shows that any semi-stable bundle has a filtration with stable quotients all of which have the same normalized Chern class. From this, together with (8.13) and the result for stable bundles, it follows that we have the equality $\Phi ( E ) = \phi ( \mu )$ for all semi-stable bundles. Finally using the canonical filtration of any $E$, (8.19) and (8.13) yield the equality in general. Thus we have now established

Proposition (8.20). If a holomorphic bundle $E$ is of type $\mu$ then for any convex invariant $\phi$ we have $\Phi ( E ) = \phi ( \mu )$, where
\[
\Phi ( E ) = \inf _ { A } \int _ { M } \phi ( ( * F ( A ) )
\]
and $A$ runs over all metric connections on $E$.
Now in $\delta 12$ we shall see that if $\mu , \nu$ are any two $n$-vectors (with $\mu _ { 1 } \geqslant \ldots \geqslant \mu _ { n }$ and $\nu _ { 1 } \geqslant \ldots \geqslant \nu _ { n }$ ) then
\[
\phi ( \mu ) = \phi ( \nu ) \text { for all convex invariant } \phi \Rightarrow \mu = \nu \text {. }
\]

In view of this we see that (8.20) amounts to a differential-geometric characterization of the type, i.e. we have
\[
E \text { is of type } \mu \text { if and only if } \Phi ( E ) = \phi ( \mu ) \text { for all convex invariant } \phi .
\]

Since $\Phi ( E )$ is defined by an infimum it follows that
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathscr { C } _ { \lambda } \text { in the closure of } \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } & \Rightarrow \Phi \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \lambda } \right) \leqslant \Phi \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } \right) \\
& \Rightarrow \phi ( \lambda ) \leqslant \phi ( \mu ) \text { by } ( 8.20 )
\end{aligned}
\]
As will be shown in § 12
\[
\phi ( \lambda ) \leqslant \phi ( \mu ) \quad \text { for all } \phi \quad \Leftrightarrow \lambda \leqslant \mu ,
\]
where $\lambda \leqslant \mu$ refers to the partial ordering (7.7). Hence we have established by differentialgeometric means the result (7.8) of Shatz.

In §5 we computed the index of the Yang-Mills functional at any critical point, and we obtained in proposition 5.4 the formula
\[
\operatorname { index } A = 2 \operatorname { dim } _ { C } H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } ^ { - } ( P ) \right) .
\]
If the critical point $A$ is of type $\mu$, so that the curvature is $- 2 \pi \mathrm { i }$ times the diagonal matrix given by $\mu$, then the holomorphic bundle $E$ determined by the connection is a direct sum
\[
E = \oplus D _ { j } ,
\]
where the $D _ { j }$ are semi-stable and have as normalized Chern classes the distinct components of $\mu$. We see therefore that the bundle $\operatorname { ad } ^ { - } ( P )$ of §5 coincides with the bundle $\operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E$ of §7, both being isomorphic (see (7.13)) to $\oplus _ { j < k } \operatorname { Hom } \left( D _ { j } , D _ { k } \right)$. Hence the index of $A$ is equal to the codimension of the stratum $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ containing $A$. In fact the normal to $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ at $A$ actually coincides with the negative eigenspace of the Hessian of $L$ at $A$, both being given by the appropriate space of harmonic forms. In view of (8.11) it follows that the codimension of $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ is equal to the Morse index of any of our functionals $\Phi$.

To sum up we see that the $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ play the role of the Morse strata not only for the Yang-Mills functional $L$ but more generally for any functional $\Phi$ defined by a strongly convex invariant function $\phi$ on the Lie algebra of $U ( n )$. This statement is to be understood in the sense that our strata $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ satisfy all the properties of (1.19) relative to $\Phi$, which in good cases, as explained in §1, characterize the Morse strata. This suggests that each critical set $\mathcal { N } _ { \mu }$ should be an equivariant deformation retract of the corresponding stratum $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$, the retraction being given by following the trajectories of $- \operatorname { grad } \Phi$. To prove this it would be enough to check it for the minimal stratum (for all $U ( n )$ ). In the coprime case $( n , k ) = 1$ this is a consequence of (8.2), but in general the singularities of $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$ give rise to difficulties and we shall not pursue this question further. Thus although we have shown that the stratification of $\mathscr { A }$ by the $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ is equivariantly perfect we have not actually proved that the Yang-Mills functional is an equivariantly perfect Morse function, although this seems very likely and would follow from sufficiently good properties about the Yang-Mills flow.

\section*{9. Cohomology of the moduli spaces}

We have now shown how to compute inductively the equivariant cohomology of the space $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } }$ of semi-stable bundles. In this section we shall show how to derive the integral cohomology of the moduli space $N ( n , k )$ in the coprime case $( n , k ) = 1$, and also that of the moduli space
$N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$ for bundles with fixed determinant. First, however, we shall need to extend theorem 7.14 by replacing the group $\mathscr { G }$ with a subgroup of finite index. As explained in §2, the group $\Gamma$ of components of $\mathscr { G }$ is $H ^ { 1 } ( M , Z ) \cong Z ^ { 2 g }$, and so a subgroup $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$ of $\mathscr { G }$ of finite index is specified by giving a sublattice of maximal rank $\Gamma ^ { \prime } \subset \Gamma$. As shown in § 2 the classifying space $B \mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$, which is a finite covering of $B \mathscr { G }$, has no torsion, it has the same Poincaré series as $B \mathscr { G }$ and $\Gamma ^ { \prime } / \Gamma ^ { \prime }$ acts trivially on its cohomology. We now consider our stratification of $\mathscr { A }$ relative to $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$. We proceed exactly as with $\mathscr { G }$. The only point to comment on is that the space $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ occurring in § 7 (namely the space of all $C ^ { \infty }$ filtrations of $E$ of type $\mu$ ) is connected. In more concrete terms this means that any two filtrations of $E$ of type $\mu$ are homotopic. To see this we note first that, over the 1-skeleton of $M$, all bundles are trivial and all filtrations homotopic (since the partial flag manifolds of $U ( n )$ are all simply connected). We can therefore collapse the 1-skeleton to a point and reduce to the case $M = S ^ { 2 }$, but now $\mathscr { G }$ becomes connected and so two filtrations of the same type, being isomorphic, are necessarily homotopic. Hence $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ is equally a homogeneous space of $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$ and if $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu } = \mathscr { G } / \mathscr { H } = \mathscr { G } ^ { \prime } / \mathscr { H } ^ { \prime }$ then $\mathscr { H } ^ { \prime } \subset \mathscr { H }$ is of finite index and corresponds to the same sublattice $\Gamma ^ { \prime }$ of $\Gamma$.

Thus our stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ is also perfect relative to $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$. In particular the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$-equivariant cohomology of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } }$ has no torsion and it is acted on trivially by $\Gamma / \Gamma ^ { \prime }$, so that the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$ and $\mathscr { G }$ Poincaré series of $\mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$ coincide.

We move on now to consider the coprime case $( n , k ) = 1$. Then stable and semi-stable coincide, so that $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } = \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } }$ and Aut $E$ acts on $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } ( E )$ with only the constant central scalars as isotropy group (Narasimhan \& Seshadri 1965). The moduli space $N ( n , k )$ is then the quotient of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } ( E )$ by this action. It is a compact complex manifold: it even inherits a natural Kähler structure as we shall see later. We want now to deduce what we can about the cohomology of $N ( n , k )$ from our general results about equivariant cohomology.

Let us denote by $\overline { \mathscr { G } }$ the quotient of $\mathscr { G }$ by its constant central $U ( 1 )$-subgroup, and similarly $\overline { \mathscr { G } } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ will be the quotient of $\mathscr { G } \mathrm { c } = \operatorname { Aut } ( E )$ by $C ^ { * }$. Thus $\overline { \mathscr { G } } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ acts freely on $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ with $N ( n , k )$ as quotient. Hence (for any coefficients)
\[
H ^ { * } ( N ( n , k ) ) \cong H _ { \mathscr { S } } ^ { * } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right)
\]
where on the right we have replaced $\overline { \mathscr { G } } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ by $\overline { \mathscr { G } } _ { \text {since } }$ they give the same cohomology. It remains to investigate the relation between $\mathscr { G }$-cohomology and $\overline { \mathscr { G } }$-cohomology. This depends on the fibration
\[
B U ( 1 ) \rightarrow B \mathscr { G } \rightarrow B \mathscr { G } ,
\]
which is always trivial in rational cohomology. This is because restriction to a point of $M$ followed by taking determinants defines a homomorphism $\mathscr { G } \rightarrow U ( 1 )$ and the composition $U ( 1 ) \rightarrow \mathscr { G } \rightarrow U ( 1 )$ is of degree $n$. This implies that
\[
H ^ { * } ( B \mathscr { G } , Q ) \rightarrow H ^ { * } ( B U ( 1 ) , Q )
\]
is surjective, which gives the triviality of the fibration over $Q$. Hence for any $\overline { \mathscr { G } }$-space $X$ the $\mathscr { G }$-Poincaré series of $X$ is the product of the $\overline { \mathscr { G } }$-Poincaré series of $X$ and $\left( 1 - t ^ { 2 } \right) ^ { - 1 }$. Together with (9.1) this then gives the formula for the Poincaré series of $N ( n , k )$ :
\[
P _ { t } ( N ( n , k ) ) = \left( 1 - t ^ { 2 } \right) \mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right)
\]
where $\mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right)$ is given inductively by theorem 7.14. As noted in the Introduction, and will be elaborated in §11, this formula coincides with that of Desale \& Ramanan (1975), which rests on the Harder-Narasimhan approach.

Taking determinants gives a natural map
\[
\operatorname { det } : N ( n , k ) \rightarrow J _ { k } ,
\]
where $J _ { k }$ is the Jacobian of $M$, parametrizing line-bundles of degree $k$. Clearly $J _ { 0 }$ acts on $N ( n , k )$ by tensor product and the determinant becomes a $J _ { 0 }$-equivariant map if we make $J _ { 0 }$ act on $J _ { k }$ via the $n$th power map
\[
\sigma _ { n } : J _ { 0 } \rightarrow J _ { 0 } .
\]
This shows that after lifting to a finite covering, with group $\operatorname { Ker } \sigma _ { n } \cong H ^ { 1 } \left( M , Z _ { n } \right) \cong \Gamma _ { n } = \Gamma / n \Gamma$, (9.4) becomes a product. Thus if we denote by $N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$ the fibre of (9.4) then
\[
N ( n , k ) = \left( N _ { 0 } ( n , k ) \times J _ { k } \right) / \Gamma _ { n } .
\]
The manifold $N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$ is the moduli space of stable bundles with fixed determinant. If we now take $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime } \subset \mathscr { G }$ corresponding to the lattice $n \Gamma \subset \Gamma$, so that $\mathscr { G } / \mathscr { G } ^ { \prime } = \Gamma / n \Gamma = \Gamma _ { n }$ then the analogue of (9.1) becomes
\[
H ^ { * } \left( N _ { 0 } ( n , k ) \times J _ { k } \right) \cong H _ { \mathscr { G } } ^ { * } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right) .
\]
Since $\mathscr { G }$ and $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$ give the same equivariant cohomology of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ (over $Q$ ) the same holds for $\overline { \mathscr { G } }$ and $\overline { \mathscr { G } } ^ { \prime }$, and so comparing (9.1) and (9.6) we get

Proposition 9.7. For rational cohomology we have
\[
H ^ { * } ( N ( n , k ) ) \cong H ^ { * } \left( N _ { 0 } ( n , k ) \right) \otimes H ^ { * } ( J )
\]
or in terms of Poincaré polynomials
\[
P _ { t } ( N ( n , k ) ) = P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( n , k ) \right) ( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g } .
\]

This proposition, which is equivalent to saying that $\Gamma _ { n }$ acts trivially on the rational cohomology of $N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$, was the main result of Harder \& Narasimhan (1975) where it was proved by numbertheoretic methods comparing $G L ( n )$ with $S L ( n )$. For us the triviality of the action of $\Gamma _ { n }$ is a consequence of its triviality on the cohomology of $B \mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$.

We turn next to the integral cohomology of the moduli space $N ( n , k )$. We want to prove that it has no torsion. We already know by (7.17) that $H _ { \mathscr { G } } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right)$ has no torsion and, in view of (9.1), we want to deduce the same result for $H _ { \overline { \mathscr { G } } } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right)$. It will be sufficient to prove that the fibration (9.2) is in fact a product so that
\[
H _ { \mathscr { G } } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right) \cong H _ { \overrightarrow { \mathscr { F } } } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right) \otimes H ( B U ( 1 ) ) .
\]
Now $B U ( 1 )$ is an Eilenberg-Maclane space $K ( Z , 2 )$ and so (9.2) has a characteristic class in $H ^ { 3 } ( B \overline { \mathscr { G } } , Z )$ whose vanishing will imply the triviality of the fibration. Equivalently we need to show that
\[
H ^ { 2 } ( B \mathscr { G } , Z ) \rightarrow H ^ { 2 } ( B U ( 1 ) , Z )
\]
is surjective, but this was the content of proposition 2.21. Thus we have now proved
Theorem 9.9. If $( n , k ) = 1$ the moduli space $N ( n , k )$ of stable bundles has no torsion.
For the space $N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$ we use the commutative diagram of fibrations
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/e9a39117-9581-4f5f-a81d-70aed9c3fba4-57.jpg?height=163&width=480&top_left_y=2265&top_left_x=691)
where $N _ { 0 } \times J \rightarrow N$ is the finite $\Gamma _ { n }$-covering. Since the bottom row has now been shown to be a product the same is true for the top row. Since we showed earlier that $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } }$ has no torsion for its $\mathscr { G } ^ { \prime }$-cohomology it follows that $N _ { 0 } \times J$ has no torsion, and hence also $N _ { 0 }$ has no torsion. Thus we have

Theorem 9.10. If $( n , k ) = 1$ the moduli space $N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$ of stable bundles with fixed determinant has no torsion.

The triviality of the fibration (9.2) when $( n , k ) = 1$ is essentially equivalent to the existence of a (topological) universal or Poincaré bundle over $M \times N$ as we shall now explain. By definition a universal bundle is a holomorphic vector bundle $V$ over $M \times N$ so that for all $n \in N$ the restriction $V _ { n }$ of $V$ to $M \times \{ n \}$ is in the isomorphism class represented by the point $n$. The projective bundle $P ( V )$ exists naturally. To see this we recall that we have an obvious holomorphic bundle $W$ over $M \times \mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$ and $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ acts holomorphically on $W$ with the constant scalars $C ^ { * }$ acting trivially on the base and as scalars in the fibre of $W$. Thus $\overline { \mathscr { G } } \overline { \mathrm { c } } = \mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } } / C ^ { * }$ acts freely on $P ( W )$ and the quotient gives $P ( V )$ over $M \times N$. A universal vector bundle is therefore a 'lift' back from this natural projective bundle over $M \times N$. If $V$ is holomorphic on each $M _ { n }$ but only continuous in $n$ we refer to it as a topological universal bundle.

Quite generally there is an obstruction to the extension of such a lift called the 'Brauer class'. It arises from the sequence
\[
1 \rightarrow C ^ { * } \rightarrow G L ( n ) \rightarrow P G L ( n ) \rightarrow 1
\]
and lies in $H ^ { 2 } \left( M \times N , \mathcal { O } ^ { * } \right)$ where $\mathcal { O } ^ { * }$ is the sheaf of multiplicative holomorphic functions. Taking the coboundary of the exponential sequence
\[
0 \longrightarrow Z \xrightarrow { \mathrm { e } ^ { 2 \pi \mathrm { i } } } \mathcal { O } \longrightarrow \mathcal { O } ^ { * } \longrightarrow 0
\]
leads to the topological Brauer class, which is an $n$-torsion class in $H ^ { 3 } ( M \times N , Z )$. Explicitly in terms of transition matrices $p _ { i j }$ for a $P G L ( n )$-bundle we lift these locally to $g _ { i j }$ in $G L ( n )$ and define the scalar $\lambda _ { i j k }$ by the formula
\[
g _ { i j } g _ { j k } = \lambda _ { i j k } g _ { i k } .
\]
This is a 2-cocycle for $\mathcal { O } ^ { * }$ and taking determinants shows that $\lambda _ { i j k } ^ { n }$ is a coboundary.
In our case since $M$ and $N$ (by (9.9)) are torsion-free it follows that the topological Brauer class must be zero and from this one can deduce that a topological universal vector bundle $V$ does indeed exist. In fact our proof of (9.9) depended essentially on the triviality of (9.2) and this in slightly disguised form is equivalent to the vanishing of the topological Brauer class, as one might suspect from the fact that the characteristic class of (9.2) is an element of $H ^ { 3 } ( B \overline { \mathscr { G } } , Z )$. To explain this we note that the bundle $W$ on $M \times \mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$ gives rise naturally to a bundle $W _ { \mathscr { G } }$ on $M \times \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \mathscr { g } }$ : this bundle is holomorphic only in the $M$-directions, since $\left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right)$ is only a topological space. Passing to the projective bundles we see that $P ( W ) _ { \overline { \mathscr { F } } }$ lifts to $P \left( W _ { \mathscr { G } } \right)$ under the natural map
\[
\pi : M \times \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \mathscr { G } } \rightarrow M \times \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \overline { \mathscr { F } } } .
\]
Thus the Brauer class of $P ( W ) _ { \overline { \mathscr { F } } }$ lifts under $\pi ^ { * }$ to the Brauer class of $P \left( W _ { \mathscr { G } } \right)$, which is zero since this bundle comes from the vector bundle $W _ { \mathscr { G } }$. On the other hand the fibration $\pi$ is trivial, since (9.2) is trivial, and so the Brauer class of $P ( W ) _ { \overline { \mathscr { E } } }$ must itself vanish. More explicitly any section $\sigma$ of $\pi$ induced by a section of (9.2) defines a vector bundle $\sigma ^ { * } W$ that has $P ( W ) _ { \overline { \mathscr { F } } }$ as projective bundle. Finally we have only to observe that, homotopically,
\[
\left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \bar { g } } \sim N
\]
and that $P ( W ) _ { \overline { \mathscr { E } } }$ corresponds (topologically) to the projective bundle $P ( W )$, while $\sigma ^ { * } W$ corresponds to a topological universal bundle $V$. This shows the tie-up between the different points of view.

In fact in this coprime case a more refined argument shows that the analytic Brauer class in $H ^ { 2 } \left( M \times N , \mathcal { O } ^ { * } \right)$ is zero, so that a holomorphic universal bundle exists. Let us recall briefly the essential point of the proof, which is to construct a holomorphic line-bundle $L$ over $M \times \mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$ on which $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ acts such that $C ^ { * } \subset \mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ acts via scalar multiplication in the fibres of $L$. Then $C ^ { * }$ acts trivially on $W \otimes L ^ { - 1 }$ and so this is acted on by the quotient group $\overline { \mathscr { G } } \mathrm { c } = \mathscr { G } \mathrm { c } / C ^ { * }$. We can now descend the bundle $W \otimes L ^ { - 1 }$ to the quotient space
\[
\left( M \times \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) / \overline { \mathscr { G } } \mathrm { c } = M \times N
\]
to obtain the universal bundle. The bundle $L$ is constructed on $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } }$ (and then lifted to $M \times \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } }$ ), by using the vector bundles given by the various $H ^ { 0 }$. More precisely if $k$ is sufficiently large (the precise values will be given later) then for any semi-stable bundle of rank $n$ and Chern class $k$ we have
\[
\begin{gathered}
H ^ { 1 } ( M , E ) = 0 \\
\operatorname { dim } H ^ { 0 } ( M , E ) = k - n ( g - 1 ) .
\end{gathered}
\]
This gives a holomorphic bundle of dimension $k - n ( g - 1 )$ over $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } }$. Taking determinants (i.e. the highest exterior power) gives a line bundle $A _ { k }$ on $\mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$. The group $C ^ { * }$ of scalar automorphisms of $E$ acts on this with weight $m = k - n ( g - 1 )$, i.e. $\lambda \in C ^ { * }$ acts by multiplication with $\lambda ^ { m }$. Tensoring $E$ with a fixed line-bundle of Chern class 1 replaces $k$ by $k + n$ so giving a line bundle $A _ { k + n }$ over $\mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$ on which $C ^ { * }$ acts with weight $m + n$. Since $( k , n ) = 1$ we have $( m , m + n ) = 1$ and we can find integers $a , b$ such that
\[
a m + b ( m + n ) = 1 .
\]
Hence $L = A _ { k } ^ { a } \otimes A _ { k + n } ^ { b }$ is acted on with weight 1 and leads to the universal bundle.
We note finally that the universal bundle is not unique and can be altered by tensoring with any holomorphic line-bundle $L$ on $M \times N$. On $M$ the universal property implies that $L$ must have degree zero (and must moreover satisfy $L ^ { n } = 1$ ) but the Chern class on $N$ is arbitrary, as is the component in $H ^ { 1 } ( M ) \otimes H ^ { 1 } ( N )$.

We recall that in §2 we proved that the integral cohomology ring $H ^ { * } ( B \mathscr { G } )$ was generated multiplicatively by certain explicit classes constructed from the canonical bundle on $M \times B \mathscr { G }$. This canonical bundle restricts to $W$ when we embed $\left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \mathscr { g } }$ in $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathscr { G } } = B \mathscr { G }$, and since our stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ is equivariantly perfect (theorem 7.14) it follows that our generators for $H ^ { * } ( B \mathscr { G } )$ restrict to give generators for $H ^ { * } \left( \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \mathscr { G } } \right)$ and hence generators for $H ^ { * } ( N )$ (after pulling back by a section $\sigma$ of the fibration $\pi$ ). Since $\sigma ^ { * } W \cong V$ (topologically), where $V$ is the universal bundle on $M \times N$ we see finally that the integral cohomology ring $H ^ { * } ( N )$ is multiplicatively generated by explicit elements constructed (as in §1) from the universal bundle $V$ on $M \times N$. These classes are of three types.
(i) The Chern classes $a _ { r }$ of $V$ restricted to $N$.
(ii) The odd-dimensional classes $b _ { r } ^ { j } ( j = 1 , \ldots , 2 g )$, which occur in the $( 1,2 r - 1 )$ Künneth component of the $r$ th Chern class of $V$ on $M \times N$.
(iii) The Chern classes $d _ { r }$ of $f _ { ! } ( V ) \in K ( N )$.

Note that in (i) and (ii) $r$ runs from 1 to $n$ while in (iii) it is unrestricted. Now since $V$ is holomorphic its $K$-theory direct image $f _ { 1 }$ may be computed directly. If $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , V _ { y } \right) = 0$ for all $y \in N$
then $f _ { ! } ( V )$ is simply the vector bundle $H$ on $N$ whose fibre at $y$ is $H ^ { 0 } \left( M , V _ { y } \right)$. Now by Serre duality $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , V _ { y } \right) = 0$ provided every homomorphism $V _ { y } \rightarrow K$ (where $K$ is the canonical line-bundle) is zero. By (7.5) this will hold provided $k / n > 2 g - 2$ in which case Riemann-Roch gives the dimension of $H ^ { 0 } \left( M , V _ { y } \right)$ as $k - n ( g - 1 )$. Now by tensoring with line-bundles we can always arrange that $k$ is of the form
\[
k = ( 2 g - 2 ) n + k ^ { \prime } \quad \text { with } \quad 0 < k ^ { \prime } < n
\]
(recall that $( k , n ) = 1$ ), so that we then have
\[
\operatorname { dim } H = n ( g - 1 ) + k ^ { \prime } .
\]

Finally then we have proved the following theorem (cf. Newstead 1972 for $n = 2$ ).
Theorem 9.11. Let $k = ( 2 g - 2 ) n + k ^ { \prime }$ with $0 < k ^ { \prime } < n$ and $( k , n ) = 1$, and let $V$ be a universal bundle over $M \times N ( n , k )$. Define integral cohomology classes $a _ { r } , b _ { r } ^ { j } , d _ { r }$ on $N$ by
\[
\begin{gathered}
a _ { r } = c _ { r } ( V \mid N ) , \quad 1 \leqslant r \leqslant n , \\
\sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { 2 g } \alpha _ { j } \otimes b _ { r } ^ { j } = c _ { r } ( V ) , \quad 1 \leqslant r \leqslant n \quad \text { and } \quad \alpha _ { j } \text { a basis for } H ^ { 1 } ( M ) , \\
d _ { r } = c _ { r } ( H ( V ) ) , \quad 1 \leqslant r \leqslant n ( g - 1 ) + k ^ { \prime }
\end{gathered}
\]
where $H ( V )$ is the bundle over $N$ whose fibre at $y$ is $H ^ { 0 } \left( M , V _ { y } \right)$. Then the integral cohomology ring of $N$ is generated by these classes.

The moduli spaces $N$ and $N _ { 0 }$ are torsion-free when $( n , k ) = 1$, and theorem 9.11 provides us with a system of integral generators $\left\{ a _ { r } , b _ { r } ^ { j } , d _ { r } \right\}$ while our Poincaré series formulae determine the dimensions of their span. Hence these rings are in principle determined once a complete set of relations for their generators is written down over the rationals. Ideally one should be able to derive these from the Thom classes of the various strata of $\mathscr { A }$, but we have been unable to make much headway in that direction, except for the computation of the fundamental group.

Note that the complex codimension $d _ { \mu }$ of any stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ other than the semi-stable stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \text {ss } }$, as given by (7.16), satisfies
\[
d _ { \mu } \geqslant 1 + ( g - 1 ) \geqslant 2 \text { if } g \geqslant 2 .
\]
This implies as in (1.12) that
\[
\pi _ { 1 } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) \cong \pi _ { 1 } ( B \mathscr { G } ) \cong H _ { 1 } ( M , Z ) .
\]
On the other hand the triviality of (9.2) shows that
\[
\pi _ { 1 } \left( \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \mathscr { G } } \right) \cong \pi _ { 1 } \left( \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) _ { \overline { \mathscr { g } } } \right) \cong \pi _ { 1 } ( N ) .
\]
Hence
\[
\pi _ { 1 } ( N ) \cong H _ { 1 } ( M , Z ) ,
\]
and this isomorphism is naturally induced by the determinant map to the Jacobian
\[
N \xrightarrow { \text { det } } J .
\]
Thus the fibre $N _ { 0 }$ of this map is simply connected. We recall that $N _ { 0 }$ is the moduli space for stable bundles with fixed determinant. Thus we have proved

Theorem 9.12. The moduli space of stable bundles of fixed determinant, and with $( n , k ) = 1$, is simply connected.

Remark. This result can also be deduced from the fact that $N _ { 0 }$ is at least uni-rational. Its rationality is conjectured but not yet proved.

Returning to the general problem of computing the relations in the cohomology ring, a careful analysis of the implications of the Riemann-Roch theorem applied to $f _ { ! }$has a good chance of succeeding, as was already noted independently by D. Mumford long before our involvement. The hope is to derive all the necessary relations from the vanishing of the Chern classes of $f _ { ! } V _ { k }$ beyond their dimension.

To provide evidence for this conjecture we shall discuss the rank two case in some detail below and roughly compare our relations with those obtained by Ramanan (1973) for genus three. Mumford is investigating this question more generally with the aid of a computer and has verified it up to genus five. But first it is expedient to make some general remarks on the normalization of the $V _ { k }$ and their relation to the tangent bundle $T$ of $N$. This material can also be found in Ramanan's paper but is considerably simpler in our context because theorem 9.11 furnishes us with integral generators that are a priori Chern classes of holomorphic line bundles. For simplicity we shall only treat the case $k = 2 n ( g - 1 ) + 1$, and write $\bar { g }$ for $g - 1$.

Recall now that $V _ { k }$ is not unique, though its projective class is. It follows that we may twist any $V _ { k }$ by the pullback $f ^ { - 1 } L$ of any holomorphic line bundle on $N$ relative to the projection
\[
M \times N \xrightarrow { f } N .
\]
Under such a twist our generators $a _ { 1 } = c _ { 1 } ( V )$ and $d _ { 1 } = c _ { 1 } \left( f _ { ! } V \right)$ change by $n c _ { 1 } ( L )$ and $( n \bar { g } + 1 ) c _ { 1 } ( L )$ respectively. Hence $\bar { g } a _ { 1 } - d _ { 1 }$ changes by $c _ { 1 } ( L )$. But $\bar { g } a _ { 1 } - d _ { 1 }$ is the Chern class of the holomorphic line-bundle $L = A _ { 1 } ^ { g } D _ { 1 } ^ { - 1 }$ where $A _ { 1 } = \operatorname { det } \left( V _ { k } \mid N \right)$ and $D _ { 1 } = \operatorname { det } f _ { 1 } ( V )$. It follows that the bundle
\[
V = V _ { k } \otimes f ^ { - 1 } L ^ { - 1 }
\]
is now determined up to isomorphism and is called the 'normalized universal bundle over $N$ '.
In what follows all our generators will be associated to this normalized $V$, so that in particular
\[
\bar { g } a _ { 1 } = d _ { 1 } .
\]

We next relate $T$ to $V$ in the $K$-theory of $N$. For each $y \in N$, the tangent space to $N$ at $y$ is canonically given by $H ^ { 1 } \left( M ; \operatorname { End } V _ { y } \right)$. Furthermore, as stable bundles admit only trivial automorphisms, $H ^ { 0 } \left( M ; \operatorname { End } V _ { y } \right) = C$. Thus in the $K$-theory of $N$
\[
1 - T = f _ { ! } ( \operatorname { End } V ) .
\]
Next observe that if, as before, $\Omega ^ { 1 }$ denotes the line bundle of holomorphic 1-forms along the fibre of $f$, then by Serre duality
\[
H ^ { 1 } \left( M ; \operatorname { End } V _ { y } \right) ^ { * } \cong H ^ { 0 } \left( M ; \operatorname { End } V _ { y } \otimes \Omega ^ { 1 } \right) ,
\]
whence
\[
T ^ { * } - 1 = f _ { ! } \left( \operatorname { End } V \otimes \Omega ^ { 1 } \right)
\]
so that subtracting these two expressions yields the relation
\[
T + T ^ { * } - 2 = f _ { ! } \left\{ \operatorname { End } V \otimes \left( \Omega ^ { 1 } - 1 \right) \right\} .
\]
As a first corollary of these relations we prove the following proposition.
Proposition 9.13 (Ramanan). The cohomology group $H ^ { 2 } \left( N _ { 0 } ; Z \right)$ is infinite cyclic and is generated by half the first Chern class of $N _ { 0 }$.

Proof. Recall that the Riemann-Roch theorem in our present simple context is given by the formula
\[
\operatorname { ch } \left( f _ { ! } W \right) = f _ { * } \{ \operatorname { ch } ( W ) \} \{ 1 - \bar { g } \omega \} ,
\]
where $\omega \in H ^ { 2 } ( M )$ is the orientation class on $M , W$ is any holomorphic bundle on $M \times N$, and $f _ { * }$ denotes integration over the fibre $M$.

Applied to End $V$ this leads to the relation
\[
- \operatorname { ch } _ { 1 } ( T ) = \operatorname { ch } _ { 1 } \left( f _ { ! } \operatorname { End } V \right) = f _ { * } \operatorname { ch } _ { 2 } ( \operatorname { End } V ) ,
\]
while with $W = V$ it yields the formula:
\[
d _ { 1 } = \operatorname { ch } _ { 1 } \left( f _ { ! } V \right) = - \bar { g } f _ { * } \left\{ \operatorname { ch } _ { 1 } ( V ) \cdot \omega \right\} + f _ { * } \left( \operatorname { ch } _ { 2 } V \right) .
\]
Here of course $\mathrm { ch } _ { i }$ denotes the part of the character of dimension $2 i$; so that in terms of the Chernclasses
\[
\mathrm { ch } _ { 1 } = c _ { 1 } \quad \mathrm { ch } _ { 2 } = \frac { 1 } { 2 } c _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } - c _ { 2 } .
\]

Now recall our definitions of the $a _ { r }$ and $b _ { r }$ as Künneth components relative to a fixed base $\alpha _ { j } \in H ^ { 1 } ( M )$. They imply the formulae
\[
c _ { r } = a _ { r } + \sum _ { j } \alpha _ { j } b _ { r } ^ { j } + f _ { r } \omega ,
\]
where $c _ { r } = c _ { r } ( V )$ and $a _ { r } , f _ { r } , b _ { r } ^ { j }$ are classes on $N$ identified with their pullback to $M \times N$. We also write
\[
c _ { r } = a _ { r } + \xi _ { r } + f _ { r } \omega
\]
for these relations, so that
\[
\xi _ { r } = \sum \alpha _ { j } b _ { r } ^ { j } .
\]
As a consequence note that the $\xi _ { r }$ are nilpotent of order three: $\xi _ { r } \xi _ { s } \xi _ { t } = 0$, and that $\xi _ { r } \omega = 0$, while $\xi _ { r } \xi _ { s }$ is a multiple of $\omega$. We therefore set
\[
\xi _ { r } ^ { 2 } = 2 \Lambda _ { r r } \omega
\]
and
\[
\xi _ { r } \xi _ { s } = \Lambda _ { r s } \omega , \quad r \neq s .
\]

In terms of the skew form $\mu _ { i j }$ given by the intersection pairing in $H ^ { 1 } ( M )$, that is
\[
\alpha _ { i } \alpha _ { j } = \mu _ { i j } \omega ,
\]
we have
\[
\Lambda _ { r r } = - \frac { 1 } { 2 } \sum \mu _ { i j } b _ { r } ^ { i } b _ { r } ^ { j } , \quad \Lambda _ { r s } = - \sum \mu _ { i j } b _ { r } ^ { i } b _ { s } ^ { j }
\]
so that these are integral non-degenerate forms in the $b _ { r }$. They are pertinent for our purposes because of the following easily proved result.

The push-forward $f _ { * } c _ { \alpha }$ of any monomial in the $c _ { i }$, is given by a universal polynomial $P _ { \alpha } ( a , \Lambda , f )$ in terms of the variables $a _ { i } , f _ { i }$ and $\Lambda _ { i j } , i \leqslant j = 1 , \ldots , n$.

For example
\[
\begin{aligned}
f _ { * } c _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } & = f _ { * } \left( a _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } + 2 a \xi + 2 f _ { 1 } \omega + 2 \Lambda _ { 11 } \omega \right) \\
& = 2 \left( a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } \right)
\end{aligned}
\]
so that by our formula for $d _ { 1 }$
\[
d _ { 1 } = - \bar { g } a _ { 1 } + a _ { 1 } f _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } - f _ { 2 } .
\]
$\operatorname { Now } f _ { 1 } = 2 n \bar { g } + 1$, as we are dealing with $V$, whence
\[
d _ { 1 } = \{ ( 2 n - 1 ) \bar { g } + 1 \} a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } - f _ { 2 } .
\]
Together with the normalization $d _ { 1 } = \bar { g } a _ { 1 }$ this yields the formula
\[
f _ { 2 } = \{ ( 2 n - 1 ) \bar { g } + 1 \} a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } ,
\]
and at this stage we are ready to compute $c _ { 1 } ( T )$. Recall first that for any $n$-dimensional bundle $V$
\[
c _ { 2 } ( \operatorname { End } V ) = ( n - 1 ) c _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } ( V ) - 2 n c _ { 2 } ( V ) .
\]

Hence
\[
\begin{aligned}
c _ { 1 } \left( f _ { ! } \operatorname { End } V \right) & = \operatorname { ch } _ { 1 } \left( f _ { ! } \operatorname { End } V \right) \\
& = - f _ { * } \left\{ ( n - 1 ) c _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } - 2 n c _ { 2 } \right\} \\
& \left. = - \left\{ 2 ( n - 1 ) a _ { 1 } f _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } \right) - 2 n f _ { 2 } \right\} \\
& = - 2 \left( a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } \right) ,
\end{aligned}
\]
so that
\[
c _ { 1 } ( T ) = 2 \left( a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } \right) .
\]

The first part of our proposition now follows by restriction to $N _ { 0 }$. The second part also follows because $a _ { 1 }$ must generate $H ^ { 2 } \left( N _ { 0 } , Z \right)$ by theorem 9.11. Note also that $\operatorname { dim } H ^ { 2 } \left( N _ { 0 } \right) = 1$ from the Poincaré series for $N _ { 0 }$.

Before proceeding to a more detailed account of the case $n = 2$, observe that if we define the total Pontryagin class $p ( T )$ of $T$ as the product of the total Chern classes of $T$ and $T ^ { * }$,
\[
p ( T ) = c ( T ) c \left( T ^ { * } \right) ,
\]
then our relation for $T + T ^ { * }$ in $K ( N )$ implies the formula
\[
p ( T ) = c \left( \operatorname { End } \mid N _ { 0 } \right) ^ { 2 \bar { g } } .
\]
This follows from the Riemann-Roch theorem, or also from the fact that the support of $\Omega ^ { 1 } - 1$ is at a point of $M$ and $c _ { 1 } \left( \Omega ^ { 1 } - 1 \right) = 2 \bar { g }$. The formula is especially simple in the rank 2 case where (cf. Newstead 1972)
\[
c ( \operatorname { End } V \mid N ) = 1 + \left( a _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } - 4 a _ { 2 } \right) .
\]
Thus the ring Pont $( T )$ generated by all the Pontryagin classes of $T$ is actually generated by the single element $\left( a _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } + 4 a _ { 2 } \right) = - p _ { 1 }$.

Newstead made two conjectures about the Pontryagin and Chern classes of $T$ : first of all that $c _ { i } ( T ) = 0$ for $i > 2 \bar { g }$, and secondly that $\operatorname { Pont } ( T ) = 0$ in $\operatorname { dim } > 4 \bar { g }$. The first of these conjectures has been recently proved by Gieseker (1982). The second conjecture remains open but, in view of our formula for $p ( T )$, is equivalent to the assertion that $p _ { 1 } ^ { g } = 0$.

We turn now finally to a more detailed examination of the relations we are after in the rank 2 case. Here we have to deal with only two Chern classes for $V$ and they are given by
\[
\begin{aligned}
& c _ { 1 } = a _ { 1 } + \xi _ { 1 } + k \omega , \quad k = 2 n \bar { g } + 1 \\
& c _ { 2 } = a _ { 2 } + \xi _ { 2 } + \left\{ ( 2 \bar { g } + 1 ) a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 } \right\} \omega ,
\end{aligned}
\]
where we have substituted for $f _ { 2 }$ the expression already found earlier. Applying the Riemann-Roch procedure one therefore obtains universal polynomials $Q _ { q } ( a ; \Lambda )$ in $\left( a _ { 1 } , a _ { 2 } , \Lambda _ { 11 } , \Lambda _ { 12 } , \Lambda _ { 22 } \right)$ such that
\[
c _ { q } \left( f _ { ! } V \right) = Q _ { q } ( a ; \Lambda ) .
\]
Hence the relations take the form
\[
Q _ { q } ( a ; \Lambda ) = 0 \quad \text { for } \quad q \geqslant 2 g \text {, }
\]
so that the 'first' of these asserts that
\[
Q _ { 2 g } ( a ; \Lambda ) = 0 .
\]

To analyse the implications of this relation further, recall the diagram
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/e9a39117-9581-4f5f-a81d-70aed9c3fba4-64.jpg?height=179&width=179&top_left_y=329&top_left_x=818)
which gave rise to the decomposition over $Q$,
\[
H ^ { * } ( N ) \simeq H ^ { * } \left( N _ { 0 } \right) \otimes H ^ { * } ( J ) ,
\]
of proposition 9.7. Note in particular that $\eta ^ { * }$ is an isomorphism over $Q$. It follows that if we introduce the new rational classes
\[
\begin{aligned}
b _ { 2 } ^ { j } = \pi ^ { * } \left( \eta ^ { * } \right) ^ { - 1 } i ^ { * } b _ { 2 } ^ { j } , & j = 1 , \ldots , 2 g , \\
a _ { k } = \pi ^ { * } \left( \eta ^ { * } \right) ^ { - 1 } i ^ { * } a _ { k } , & k = 1,2 ,
\end{aligned}
\]
then these will generate $\pi ^ { * } H ^ { * } \left( N / J _ { 0 } \right)$ so that as a $\Lambda ^ { * } \left( b _ { 1 } ^ { j } \right)$-module, $H ^ { * } ( N )$ is freely generated by their span.

In terms of these variables, and the corresponding $\Lambda$, our polynomial $Q _ { g } ( a , \Lambda )$ is now transformed into a polynomial $R _ { g } ( \boldsymbol { a } ; \boldsymbol { \Lambda } )$ and if this expression is expanded in terms of the basis
\[
b _ { 1 } ^ { I } = b _ { 1 } ^ { i _ { 1 } } \ldots b _ { 1 } ^ { i _ { k } } , \quad i _ { 1 } < i _ { 2 } < \ldots < i _ { k }
\]
for $\Lambda ^ { * } \left( b _ { 1 } ^ { j } \right)$, then $R _ { g } ( \boldsymbol { a } , \boldsymbol { \Lambda } ) = 0$ implies that each coefficient in this expansion must vanish. Equivalently one can multiply $R _ { g }$ by $b _ { 1 } ^ { I }$ and integrate over the fibre of $\pi$, to obtain
\[
\pi _ { * } b _ { 1 } ^ { I } R _ { g } ( a ; \Lambda ) = 0 ,
\]
yielding a large number of relations in $a _ { 1 } , a _ { 2 }$ and $b _ { 2 } ^ { i }$.
To carry out this process one first of all has to determine the old generators in terms of the new bold-faced ones, and this is done quite easily by observing that $\operatorname { End } V$ descends to $N / J _ { 0 }$ so that the characteristic classes of $\operatorname { End } V$ certainly are in the image of $\pi ^ { * }$. Thus $a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 11 }$ is in the image of $\pi ^ { * }$ and restricts to $i ^ { * } a _ { 1 }$ on $N _ { 0 }$ whence
\[
a _ { 1 } = a _ { 1 } + \Lambda _ { 1 } \quad \text { or } \quad a _ { 1 } = a _ { 1 } - \Lambda _ { 11 } .
\]
Similarly $p _ { 1 } ( T )$ is in this image. Hence
\[
\pi ^ { * } \left( \eta ^ { * } \right) ^ { - 1 } i ^ { * } \left( a _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } - 4 a _ { 2 } \right) = a _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } - 4 a _ { 2 } .
\]
On the other hand as $i ^ { * } , \eta ^ { * }$ and $\pi ^ { * }$ are ring-homomorphisms the expression on the left is also equal to $\boldsymbol { a } _ { 1 } ^ { 2 } - \mathbf { 4 } \boldsymbol { a } _ { 2 }$. Eliminating one obtains:
\[
a _ { 2 } = a _ { 2 } - \frac { 1 } { 2 } \Lambda _ { 11 } a _ { 1 } + \frac { 1 } { 4 } \Lambda _ { 11 } ^ { 2 } .
\]

Finally to determine $\boldsymbol { b } _ { \mathbf { 2 } } ^ { i }$ consider $c _ { \mathbf { 2 } } ( \operatorname { End } V ) = c _ { \mathbf { 1 } } ^ { 2 } - \mathbf { 4 } c _ { \mathbf { 2 } }$, whose Künneth components must again all be in the image of $\pi ^ { * }$. Applied to the first mixed component this yields the result that
\[
\left( 2 a , b _ { 1 } ^ { i } - 4 b _ { 2 } ^ { i } \right)
\]
is in the image of $\pi ^ { * }$, whence
\[
b _ { 2 } ^ { i } = b _ { 2 } ^ { i } + \frac { 1 } { 2 } \left( a _ { 1 } - \Lambda _ { 11 } \right) b _ { 1 } ^ { i } .
\]

Let us now expand our first relation $R _ { g } ( \boldsymbol { a } , \boldsymbol { \Lambda } )$ in terms of $\Lambda _ { 11 }$ and $\Lambda _ { 12 }$, when $g = \mathbf { 3 }$. Also, in part to avoid subscripts and in part to come closer to Ramanan's notation, let us set
\[
h = a _ { 1 } , \quad \nu = a _ { 2 } , \quad \theta = \Lambda _ { 22 }
\]
and
\[
\omega = \Lambda _ { 11 } , \quad \Delta = \Lambda _ { 12 } .
\]
Then for dimensional reasons the possible monomials in $\omega$ and $\Delta$ occurring in $R _ { 3 }$ are given by the following table:

