\hline 6 & - & - & - & $\omega ^ { 3 }$ & - & - & $\Delta ^ { 2 } \omega ^ { 2 }$ \\
\hline 5 & - & - & - & - & $\Delta \omega ^ { 2 }$ & - & - \\
\hline 4 & - & - & $\omega ^ { 2 }$ & - & - & $\Delta ^ { 2 } \omega$ & - \\
\hline 3 & - & - & - & $\Delta \omega$ & - & - & $\Delta ^ { 3 }$ \\
\hline 2 & - & $\omega$ & - & - & $\Delta ^ { 2 }$ & - & - \\
\hline 1 & - & - & $\Delta$ & - & - & - & - \\
\hline 0 & - & - & - & - & - & - & - \\
\hline & 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 10 & 12 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
Here the dimension of a term relative to the $b _ { 1 } ^ { j }$ is indicated on the left and the total dimension below. The total dimension of $R _ { 3 }$ is 12 so that the coefficient of one of these monomials (which is a polynomial in $h , \nu$ and $\theta$ ) must make up for the deficiency in the dimension. Note further that every element in the top row must be a multiple of $b _ { 1 } ^ { 1 } \wedge \ldots \wedge b _ { 1 } ^ { 6 } = \frac { 1 } { 6 } \omega ^ { 6 }$. Hence $\Delta ^ { 2 } \omega ^ { 2 }$ is also such a multiple and, as is easily checked, is in fact given by
\[
\Delta ^ { 2 } \omega ^ { 2 } = \text { const. } \times \theta ^ { 2 } \omega ^ { 3 } .
\]
In short then, the expression $R _ { 3 }$ takes the form
\[
R _ { 3 } = A _ { 6 } \omega ^ { 3 } + B _ { 4 } \Delta \omega ^ { 2 } + C _ { 8 } \omega ^ { 2 } + D _ { 2 } \Delta ^ { 2 } \omega + E _ { 0 } \Delta ^ { 3 } + F _ { 6 } \Delta \omega + \ldots .
\]
The first two relations now follow immediately. We must have
\[
A _ { 6 } = 0 \quad \text { and } \quad B _ { 4 } b _ { 2 } ^ { i } = 0 , \quad i = 1 , \ldots , n .
\]

At the next level more care has to be taken as the two terms interfere. One procedure is to write down the implications $\pi _ { * } \omega R _ { g } = 0$, and $\left( \pi _ { * } u \right) R _ { g } = 0$ with $u \in \Lambda ^ { 2 } \left( b _ { 1 } ^ { 1 } , \ldots , b _ { 1 } ^ { n } \right)$, subject to $u \omega ^ { 2 } = 0$. The first of these leads to a relation of the form
\[
C _ { 8 } + \text { const. } \times \theta _ { 2 } D _ { 2 } = 0
\]
while the second one implies that
\[
D _ { 2 } x = 0 \quad \text { for all } \quad x \in \Lambda ^ { 2 } \left( b _ { 2 } ^ { 1 } , \ldots , b _ { 2 } \right) \quad \text { of the form } \quad \pi _ { * } u \Delta ^ { 2 } , \text { with } u \omega ^ { 2 } = 0 .
\]
In short then, in these and subsequent relations the decomposition of $\Lambda ^ { * } \left( b _ { \mathbf { 1 } } ^ { i } \right)$ into primitive classes relative to $\omega$ makes its appearance, and as is really not too surprising this decomposition corresponds under $\pi _ { * }$ to the corresponding decomposition under $\theta$ in $\Lambda ^ { * } \left( b _ { 2 } ^ { 1 } , \ldots , b _ { 2 } ^ { n } \right)$. Thus the last relation is equivalent to
\[
D _ { 2 } x = 0 \text { for } x \theta ^ { 2 } = 0 .
\]
Similarly the $E _ { 0 } \Delta ^ { 3 }$ term produces the relation
\[
E _ { 0 } x = 0 \quad \text { for } \quad x \in \Lambda ^ { 3 } \left( b _ { 2 } ^ { i } \right) \quad \text { with } \quad x \theta = 0 .
\]

We have traced the nature of these five relations so carefully because they correspond precisely to the complete set of relations actually found by Ramanan in this case using a quite different method. Here are his relations:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] $3 h ^ { 3 } - 10 h \nu - 4 \theta = 0$,
\item[(2)] $\left( h ^ { 2 } - 2 \nu \right) V = 0 \quad \left( V = \operatorname { span } \right.$ of $\left. b _ { 2 } ^ { j } \right)$,
\end{itemize}
\begin{itemize}
\item[(3)] $\left( h ^ { 2 } - 3 \nu \right) \nu = 0$,
\item[(4)] $h x = 0$, for $x \in \Lambda ^ { 2 } V$, with $x \theta ^ { 2 } = 0$,
\item[(5)] $y = 0$, for $y \in \Lambda ^ { 3 } V$, with $y \theta = 0$.
\end{itemize}

This concludes our remarks on the relations among the generators of theorem 9.11. Clearly the computations involved in carrying out the programme laid out here are quite astronomical and therefore appropriately best left to a computer. Note that in the present case of genus 3 the single equation $c _ { 2 g } \left( f _ { 1 } V \right) = 0$ implied all the necessary relations. On the other hand it is disappointing, and shows how deep Newstead's conjectures lie, that even with all the relations before one, the formulae $p _ { 1 } ( T ) ^ { g } = 0$ and $c _ { i } ( T ) = 0$ for $i \geqslant 2 \bar { g }$ are by no means obvious.

Finally we revert to the geometry of the moduli space $N ( n , k )$ and show that it inherits a natural Kähler structure. The essential observation is that the space $\mathscr { A }$ of unitary connections has a natural symplectic structure: if $\alpha , \beta$ are two ad $( P )$-valued 1-forms on $M$ they have a skew product $\int _ { M } \alpha \wedge \beta$ (we recall that this uses the inner product in the Lie algebra). This symplectic structure is preserved by the action of $\mathscr { G }$. Moreover the curvature
\[
F : \mathscr { A } \rightarrow \Omega ^ { 2 } ( M ; \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )
\]
can be identified with the corresponding moment map. To see this we first note that $\Omega ^ { 2 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$ is canonically dual to $\Omega ^ { 0 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$, which is the Lie algebra of $\mathscr { G }$. Hence for any $\phi \in \Omega ^ { 0 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$ we have a real-valued function $F _ { \phi }$ on $\mathscr { A }$, defined by $F _ { \phi } ( A ) = \left( F _ { A } , \phi \right)$. To say that $F$ is the moment map for the $\mathscr { G }$-action on $\mathscr { A }$ means that the Hamiltonian vector field on $\mathscr { A }$ defined by $F _ { \phi }$ coincides with the vector field given by the Lie algebra action of $\phi$. Equivalently we have to show that, for any $\psi \in \Omega ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$,
\[
\left( \mathrm { d } F _ { \phi } , \psi \right) = \int \left( \mathrm { d } _ { A } \phi \right) \wedge \psi .
\]
But, as we saw in §3, the left-hand side is equal to $\int \left( \mathrm { d } _ { A } \psi \right) \wedge \phi$. Since $\mathrm { d } _ { A }$ is a derivation and $\int \mathrm { d } ( \psi \wedge \phi ) = 0$ we have the usual formula for integration by parts
\[
\int \left( \mathrm { d } _ { A } \psi \right) \wedge \phi = - \int \psi \wedge \left( \mathrm { d } _ { A } \phi \right)
\]
which verifies (9.14).
The constant central $U ( 1 )$ subgroup of $\mathscr { G }$ acts trivially on $\mathscr { A }$ corresponding to the fact that the function $\int \operatorname { trace } F _ { A }$ is constant and equal to $- 2 \pi \mathrm { i } k$ (where $k$ is the Chern class).

The moment map is $\mathscr { G }$-equivariant and so to every orbit $C \subset \Omega ^ { 2 } ( M , \operatorname { ad } ( P ) )$ the inverse image $F ^ { - 1 } ( C ) \subset \mathscr { A }$ is $\mathscr { G }$-invariant. The quotient $F ^ { - 1 } ( C ) / \mathscr { G }$ is sometimes called the Marsden-Weinstein quotient. Under appropriate non-degeneracy conditions, it is a manifold and it inherits a natural symplectic structure from that of $\mathscr { A }$. In particular taking $C$ to be the orbit given by the Yang-Mills minimum (i.e. the constant conjugacy class with all eigenvalues $- 2 \pi \mathrm { i } k / n$ ) we obtain the moduli space $N ( n , k )$. Thus $N ( n , k )$ inherits a natural symplectic structure.

The symplectic structure on $\mathscr { A }$ together with its natural metric defines the complex structure of $\mathscr { C }$. Similarly the induced symplectic structure and metric on $N$ define its complex structure. Thus $N$ is a Kähler manifold.

Note that the tangent space to $N$ at $E$ is $H ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { End } E )$ and it is easy to define the metric, complex structure and symplectic structure on this tangent space. What is not immediately clear is the global integrability condition of the complex and symplectic structures so defined on $N$. The complex structure becomes clear by expressing $N$ as the quotient $\mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } / \operatorname { Aut } ( E )$ while the symplectic structure is similarly transparent as the 'Marsden-Weinstein quotient'.

\section*{10. The stratification for general $G$}

In this section we shall indicate briefly how to extend the results of the previous sections from $U ( n )$ to general compact Lie groups $G$. We shall content ourselves now with the basic results about rational cohomology, since the presence of torsion in $G$ makes it difficult to say much in general about integral cohomology.

On the algebro-geometric side the work of Narasimhan-Seshadri has been extended to general reductive groups by Ramanathan (1975). We shall, however, adopt a slightly different, although equivalent, approach to stability and the canonical filtration, reducing everything to the vector bundle case by a systematic use of the adjoint representation.

The general set-up is much the same as before and we shall use the same notation. Thus we start with a given $C ^ { \infty }$ principal $G$-bundle $P$ over $M$ and we denote by $\mathscr { A }$ the space of connections and $\mathscr { G }$ the group of automorphisms. It is again true that a connection on $P$ defines a holomorphic structure on $P ^ { \mathrm { c } }$, the associated bundle with group $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ the complexification of $G$. Conversely a holomorphic $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle together with a reduction of structure group to $G$ determines a canonical $G$-connection (Singer 1959) so that we may identify $\mathscr { A }$ with the space of holomorphic structures on $P ^ { \mathrm { c } }$.

To proceed further we need to introduce the appropriate stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ by strata $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ analogous to the Harder-Narasimhan stratification for the case of $G L ( n )$. We shall in fact define such a stratification by using the canonical filtration of the vector bundle ad $\left( P ^ { c } \right)$ in an appropriate way. First of all, however, we need a few lemmas concerning vector bundles.

We have already noted in § 8 that a semi-stable vector bundle of slope (or normalized Chern class) $\mu$ has a filtration with stable quotients of slope $\mu$. The converse is also true in view of lemma 7.5. This enables us to extend results for stable bundles to semi-stable bundles by induction. In this way we shall prove

Lemma 10.1. If $E , F$ are semi-stable of slopes $\mu , \nu$ then $E \otimes F$ is semi-stable of slope $\mu + \nu$.
Proof. Consider the first case when $E , F$ are both stable. According to the Narasimhan-Seshadri theorem 8.1 they then arise from unitary representations of the extended fundamental group $\Gamma _ { R }$ (as in § 6) with slopes $\mu , v$. The tensor product $E \otimes F$ then arises from the tensor product of the two unitary representations. This tensor product is not necessarily irreducible but, being unitary, it is a direct sum of irreducible pieces. Moreover the slope of a representation is given by the character of the centre of $\Gamma _ { R }$ and this therefore takes the same value $\mu + \nu$ on all the irreducible pieces. Hence $E \otimes F$ is a direct sum of stable bundles of slope $\mu + \nu$ and so is semi-stable and of the same slope. Now we move on to the general case and use filtrations of $E$ and $F$ with stable quotients $D _ { j } , G _ { k }$ respectively. The tensor product then inherits a filtration with quotients $D _ { j } \otimes G _ { k }$, which as we have just proved are stable and of slope $\mu + v$. Hence $E \otimes F$ is semi-stable and of slope $\mu + \nu$.

For our next lemmas, which concern general vector bundles $E$, it will be convenient to introduce some additional notation. Let
\[
0 = E _ { 0 } \subset E _ { 1 } \subset E _ { 2 } \subset \ldots \subset E _ { r }
\]
be the canonical filtration of $E$ with semi-stable quotients
\[
D _ { j } = E _ { j } / E _ { j - 1 } , \quad \operatorname { slope } \left( D _ { j } \right) = \mu _ { j } , \quad \mu _ { 1 } > \mu _ { 2 } > \ldots > \mu _ { r } .
\]

We shall write
\[
\inf E = \mu _ { r } , \quad \sup E = \mu _ { 1 } .
\]
Thus $E$ is semi-stable if and only if $\inf E = \sup E$.
The next lemma is then a straightforward consequence of lemma 7.5 , with double induction on the steps of the canonical filtrations.

Lemma 10.2. $\inf E \geqslant \mu$ if and only if, for every $F$ with $\sup F < \mu$, every homomorphism $F \rightarrow E$ is zero.
Using (10.1) and (10.2) and again using double induction one obtains
Lemma 10.3. $\inf ( E \otimes F ) = \inf E + \inf F$.
With these vector bundle lemmas out of the way we return to consider a holomorphic $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ bundle $\xi$ over $M$. Let $E = \operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$ be the vector bundle associated with the adjoint representation, so that $E$ is actually a holomorphic bundle of Lie algebras. Since the Lie algebra of $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ has a nondegenerate invariant quadratic form so does the bundle $E$. In particular $E$ is self-dual so that its canonical filtration must be of the form
\[
0 \subset E _ { - r } \subset E _ { - r + 1 } \subset \ldots \subset E _ { - 1 } \subset E _ { 0 } \subset E _ { 1 } \subset \ldots \subset E _ { r - 1 } \subset E ,
\]
where $E _ { - j }$ is the polar space (relative to the quadratic form) of $E _ { j - 1 }$. We have indexed things in such a way that $D _ { 0 } = E _ { 0 } / E _ { - 1 }$ has slope zero. Since $E _ { - 1 }$ is the polar space of $E _ { 0 }$ we have an induced non-degenerate quadratic form on $D _ { 0 }$.

Consider now the Lie bracket
\[
\phi : E _ { 0 } \otimes E _ { 0 } \rightarrow E / E _ { 0 } .
\]
Since $\inf \left( E _ { 0 } \otimes E _ { 0 } \right) = 0$, by (10.3), and $\sup E / E _ { 0 } < 0$, lemma 10.2 implies that $\phi = 0$. Hence $E _ { 0 }$ is a Lie sub-algebra bundle of $E$. For similar reasons
\[
\left[ E _ { - j } , E _ { - 1 } \right] \subset E _ { - j - 1 } \text { for } j \geqslant 0
\]
so that $E _ { - 1 }$ is a nilpotent ideal: it is the nilpotent radical and $D _ { 0 }$ the reductive quotient of $E _ { 0 }$. It now follows (see lemma below) that $E _ { \mathbf { 0 } }$ is a parabolic sub-algebra bundle, i.e. it contains (over every point of $M$ ) a maximal solvable (Borel) sub-algebra. Now a parabolic sub-algebra generates a parabolic subgroup and this is its own normalizer. Hence the sub-algebra bundle $E _ { 0 } \subset \operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$ determines a reduction of the structure group of $\xi$ to this parabolic subgroup $Q$. We denote this new principal bundle by $\xi _ { Q }$ and call it the canonical parabolic reduction of $\xi$.

For $G ^ { \mathrm { c } } = G L ( n , C )$ the parabolic subgroups are the stabilizers of partial flags and a parabolic reduction of the principal bundle is equivalent to giving a filtration of the associated vector bundle. We shall now show that the canonical parabolic reduction defined above does indeed coincide with the canonical filtration of Harder-Narasimhan. So let $V$ be a holomorphic vector bundle and let
\[
0 = V _ { 0 } \subset V _ { 1 } \subset V _ { 2 } \subset \ldots \subset V _ { r } = V
\]
be its canonical filtration so that the associated quotients
\[
W _ { j } = V _ { j } / V _ { j - 1 }
\]
are semi-stable and have slopes $\mu _ { j }$ strictly decreasing with $j$. The adjoint bundle $E$ is now $\operatorname { End } V$. The filtration of $V$ induces a filtration
\[
0 = E ^ { - r } \subset \ldots \subset E ^ { - 1 } \subset E ^ { 0 } \subset E ^ { 1 } \subset \ldots \subset E ^ { r - 1 } ,
\]
where $\phi$ is in $E ^ { j }$ provided $\phi \left( V _ { i } \right) \subset V _ { i + j }$ for all $i$. Thus $E ^ { 0 }$ consists precisely of endomorphisms preserving the filtration of $V$ so that
\[
E ^ { 0 } / E ^ { - 1 } \cong \oplus _ { i } \operatorname { End } V _ { i } .
\]
More generally
\[
E ^ { j } / E ^ { j - 1 } \cong \oplus _ { i } \operatorname { Hom } \left( V _ { i } , V _ { i + j } \right) .
\]
In view of (10.1) it follows that $E ^ { 0 } / E ^ { - 1 }$ is semi-stable of slope 0 while
\[
\inf E ^ { - 1 } > 0 \quad \sup E / E ^ { 0 } < 0 .
\]
Comparing the filtration $E ^ { j }$ of $E$ with the canonical filtration and using (10.2) it easily follows that $E ^ { 0 }$ coincides with $E _ { 0 }$ and $E ^ { - 1 }$ with $E _ { - 1 }$. Thus the two parabolic reductions do in fact agree.

Remark. Note that the filtration $E ^ { j }$ does not totally coincide with the canonical filtration $E _ { j }$ : it has to be further refined depending on the particular sequence of slopes before it does so.

Next we shall prove
Proposition 10.4. The canonical parabolic reduction is functorial with respect to group homomorphisms.
Thus let $\phi : G ^ { \mathrm { c } } \rightarrow H ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ be a homomorphism, $\xi$ a principal $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle, and $\eta = \phi ( \xi )$ the associated $H ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle. Then we have a homomorphism of Lie-algebra bundles
\[
\phi : \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) \rightarrow \operatorname { ad } ( \eta ) .
\]
Since $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ is reductive the homomorphism of Lie algebras induced by $\phi$ has $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-invariant complements to the kernel and image. Hence putting $E = \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) , F = \operatorname { ad } ( \eta )$ we can decompose $\phi : E \rightarrow F$ into split exact sequences
\[
0 \rightarrow K \rightarrow E \rightarrow I \rightarrow 0 , \quad 0 \rightarrow I \rightarrow F \rightarrow J \rightarrow 0 .
\]
Now for any direct sum $A \oplus B$ of vector bundles it is easy to see, using 10.3, that the parts of the canonical filtration with slope $\geqslant 0$ are additive: $( A \oplus B ) _ { 0 } = A _ { 0 } \oplus B _ { 0 }$. Applying this to our situation we see that
\[
E _ { 0 } \cong K _ { 0 } \oplus I _ { 0 } , \quad F _ { 0 } \cong I _ { 0 } \oplus J _ { 0 }
\]
so that $\phi$ sends $E _ { 0 }$ into $F _ { 0 }$. This proves that the canonical parabolic reduction of $\eta$ is induced by that of $\xi$.

For a vector bundle we defined its type $\mu$ in terms of the Chern classes of the semi-stable quotients of its canonical filtration. We shall introduce the corresponding notion for a general group. Thus let $\xi$ be a principal $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle, $\xi _ { Q }$ its canonical parabolic reduction. To every character $\chi$ of $Q$ (i.e. a homomorphism $\chi : Q \rightarrow C ^ { * }$ ) we have a line-bundle $\chi \left( \xi _ { Q } \right)$ over $M$ and so an integer Chern class $c _ { 1 } \chi \left( \xi _ { Q } \right)$. In this way we obtain a homomorphism
\[
\hat { Q } \rightarrow Z ,
\]
where $\hat { Q }$ is the abelian group of characters of $Q$. This will essentially be our type. To see more clearly what it involves let us pass to the reductive quotient $S$ of $Q$, i.e. the quotient by its unipotent radical $R$ (maximal connected normal unipotent subgroup). For $G L ( n )$ we have
\[
S = G L \left( n _ { 1 } \right) \times \ldots \times G L \left( n _ { r } \right)
\]
where the $n _ { j }$ are the dimensions of the quotients in the canonical filtration. Clearly $\hat { Q } = \hat { S }$ so that for $G L ( n )$ the homomorphism $\hat { Q } \rightarrow Z$ consists precisely of assigning the Chern classes $k _ { 1 } , \ldots , k _ { r }$ to appropriate semi-stable factors. The general case is similar in that $\widehat { S }$ is a lattice of rank equal to
the dimension of the centre of $S$ and the type will then be a vector in the dual lattice. Now for $G L ( n )$ we found it convenient to replace the sequence of $\left( n _ { j } , k _ { j } \right)$ by a single $n$-vector $\mu$ and we shall reinterpret our type in a similar way for the general case.

The group $Q$ is the semi-direct product $R S$. In fact $S$ can be identified with the complexification $K ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ of the maximal compact subgroup of $K$. If $T _ { 0 }$ is the connected component of the centre then a character of $S$ defines a (unitary) character of $T _ { 0 }$ and the map $\widehat { S } \rightarrow \widehat { T } _ { 0 }$ is injective with finite cokernel. Now we may assume $K \subset G$ and that the maximal torus $T _ { 1 }$ of $K$ is contained in the maximal torus $T$ of $G$. Passing to characters gives surjective maps
\[
\hat { T } _ { \rightarrow } \hat { T } _ { 1 } \rightarrow \hat { T } _ { 0 }
\]
while taking $\operatorname { Hom } ( , Z )$ gives an inclusion of the corresponding dual lattices
\[
L _ { 0 } \subset L _ { 1 } \subset L .
\]
Each lattice here can be identified with the integral points in the Lie algebra of the corresponding torus (i.e. the kernel of $\exp 2 \pi \mathrm { i }$ ). The lattice $\operatorname { Hom } ( \widehat { S } , Z )$ then contains $L _ { 0 }$ as a sublattice of finite index. In particular we may view Hom $( \widehat { S } , Z )$ as a subgroup of the Lie algebra of $T$. In this way the type of our $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle $\xi$ becomes an element $\mu$ of the Lie algebra of $T$.

For $G L ( n , C )$ our vector $\mu$ satisfied the inequalities
\[
\mu _ { 1 } \geqslant \mu _ { 2 } \geqslant \ldots \geqslant \mu _ { n } ,
\]
which describe a fundamental chamber for the action of the Weyl group $W$. The choice of this chamber derived from the parabolic subgroup determined by the complex structure. From the unitary point of view $\mu$, or rather its $W$-orbit, corresponds naturally to a conjugacy class in the Lie algebra of $U ( n )$ and this (up to $2 \pi \mathrm { i }$ ) is the curvature of the Yang-Mills connection associated with $\mu$. In this way we saw that each stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ contains a unique component of the Yang-Mills connection. For general groups the situation is now exactly the same: $\mu$ determines a conjugacy class in the Lie algebra of $G$ and hence a component of the Yang-Mills connection. The group $K$ is the local holonomy group and this determines the integrality conditions on $\mu$.

The stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$, consisting of all $\xi$ of given type $\mu$, has a conormal bundle whose fibre at $\xi$ is $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) / E _ { 0 } \right)$ where $E _ { 0 }$ is as above the canonical parabolic subalgebra of $\operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$.

If $\rho : G \rightarrow H$ is a homomorphism and $\xi$ is a $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle of type $\mu$ then proposition 10.4 implies that $\rho ( \xi )$ is an $H ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle of type $\rho ( \mu )$. Here $\mu$ and $\rho ( \mu )$ are best considered as conjugacy classes of the appropriate Lie algebras. Even if $\rho$ is an embedding $\rho ( \mu )$ does not necessarily determine $\mu$, but the Peter-Weyl theorem implies that if $\rho ( \mu ) = \rho ( \nu )$ for all unitary representations $\rho$ of $G$ then $\mu = \nu$. Thus we have

Proposition 10.6. A $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle $\xi$ is of type $\mu$ if and only if $\rho ( \xi )$ is of type $\rho ( \mu )$ for all unitary representations $\rho$ of $G$.

This proposition, together with (7.8), which describes the closure properties of the strata for $U ( n )$, enables us to derive similar results for every $G$. Thus let $\mathscr { C } _ { \lambda }$ be a stratum for $G$ lying in the closure of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$. Then (10.6) implies that $\rho \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \lambda } \right) = \mathscr { C } _ { \rho ( \lambda ) }$ lies in the closure of $\mathscr { C } _ { \rho ( \mu ) }$ for all $\rho$. Hence by (7.8) we have $\rho ( \lambda ) \geqslant \rho ( \mu )$ for the partial ordering on conjugacy classes of $\mathfrak { u } ( n )$. But by (12.18) this is equivalent to $\lambda \geqslant \mu$ where this is the partial ordering on conjugacy classes of $\mathfrak { g }$ defined in § 12 (and corresponds to inclusion of convex hulls). Thus (7.8) holds for all $G$.

As with the unitary case the conormal to the stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ at $\xi$ can be identified with
\[
H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) / E _ { 0 } \right)
\]
where $E _ { 0 }$ gives the canonical parabolic reduction of $\xi$. Since $\inf \left( \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) / E _ { 0 } \right) < 0$ it follows that $H ^ { 0 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) / E _ { 0 } \right) = 0$ and by Riemann-Roch we can compute the complex codimension $d _ { \mu }$ of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$. One finds the following generalization of (7.15):
\[
d _ { \mu } = \sum _ { \alpha ( \mu ) > 0 } \{ \alpha ( \mu ) + g - 1 \} ,
\]
where $\alpha$ runs over the positive roots of $G$, and $\mu$ is the representative in the positive Weyl chamber (so that $\alpha ( \mu ) \geqslant 0$ ). In particular we see that
\[
d _ { \mu } = 0 \Leftrightarrow \mu \text { is in the centre. }
\]
In this case $\mu$ is uniquely determined by the topology of $\xi$ (there will be one Chern class for each circle factor in the centre). We define this stratum to be the semi-stable stratum: it is necessarily open and non-empty. Moreover $\mu$ is central if and only if $\operatorname { ad } ( \mu ) = 0$ so that we have in this case the following strengthening of (10.6).

Proposition 10.9. A $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle $\xi$ is semi-stable if and only if $\operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$ is a semi-stable vector bundle.
As with the unitary case a general stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ is equivariantly equivalent to a semi-stable stratum for the group $K$. Moreover the connected centre $T _ { 0 }$ of $K$ acts on $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) / E _ { 0 } \right)$ with no trivial character: in fact the connected centralizer of $T _ { 0 }$ in $G ^ { \mathrm { e } }$ is just $K ^ { \mathrm { e } }$.

We now have all the ingredients to deduce as in (7.14)
Theorem 10.10. For any $G$ the stratification of $\mathscr { C }$ by the $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ is equivariantly perfect over the rationals so that for Poincaré series we have
\[
P _ { t } ( \mathscr { C } ) = \sum _ { \mu } t ^ { 2 d _ { \mu } } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } \right)
\]
where $d _ { \mu }$ is given by (10.8).
In principle this enables us to calculate the equivariant cohomology of the semi-stable stratum by induction on the dimension of $G$. The point is that, for any other stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$, the equivariant cohomology is equal to that of a semi-stable stratum for a proper subgroup $K$ of $G$, namely the maximal compact subgroup of the parabolic subgroup of $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ determined by $\mu$. When $G = U ( n )$ the group $K$ is always of the form $U \left( n _ { 1 } \right) \times \ldots \times U \left( n _ { r } \right)$ and so our induction in the unitary case did not use other groups. However, for general $G$ the groups $K$ that occur are centralizers of tori and can be of many types.

To relate this to the Morse theory for the Yang-Mills functional $L$ we note first that, after suitable normalization $L$ is functorial for homomorphisms of Lie groups. Hence (10.6) together with (8.13) enables us to deduce, for any $G$,
\[
A \text { of type } \mu \Rightarrow L ( A ) \geqslant L ( \mu ) = | \mu | ^ { 2 } .
\]
On the other hand our description of Yang-Mills connections shows that every stratum $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ does in fact contain a critical set $\mathscr { N } _ { \mu }$, so that, on $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu } , L ( A )$ achieves its minimum. To go further and establish the generalization of (8.20) we need the following lemma, in which $\Gamma _ { R }$ denotes the central extension of $\pi _ { 1 } ( M )$ by $R$ defined in § 6.

Lemma 10.12. A holomorphic $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle $\xi$ arises from a homomorphism $\rho : \Gamma _ { R } \rightarrow G$ if and only if $\operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$ arises from a unitary representation of $\Gamma _ { R }$.

Proof. In one direction this is trivial. For the converse let $\operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$ arise from a unitary representation of $\Gamma _ { R }$, and consider the Lie bracket homomorphism of vector bundles:
\[
\operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) \otimes \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) \rightarrow \operatorname { ad } ( \xi ) .
\]

Both sides are vector bundles arising from unitary representations of $\Gamma _ { R }$ and, as proved by Narasimhan \& Seshadri (1965), this implies the homomorphism is covariant constant. This means that $\operatorname { ad } ( \xi )$ as an $\operatorname { ad } ( G )$-bundle comes from a homomorphism $\pi _ { 1 } ( M ) \rightarrow \operatorname { ad } ( G )$. It is then easy, using the theory of line-bundles, to lift this to a homomorphism $\rho : \Gamma _ { R } \rightarrow G$, which will, on extension to $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$, define $\xi$.

Arguing along the lines of § 8 and using (10.12) one can then prove
Proposition 10.13. If a holomorphic $G ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-bundle $\xi$ is of type $\mu$ then
\[
\inf _ { A } L ( A ) = L ( \mu ) ,
\]
where $A$ runs over all compact connections on $\xi$.
Quite likely (10.11) and (10.13) hold for all convex invariant functions on the Lie algebra of $G$ : they certainly do for any $\phi$ induced from a representation.

To sum up therefore we see that the picture for general $G$ is in practically all respects similar to the unitary case, with the notable difference that we have had to switch from integral to rational cohomology.

\section*{11. Comparison with Harder-Narasimhan approach}

As mentioned in the Introduction the Poincaré polynomials of the moduli spaces of stable bundles have been computed by number-theory methods in Harder \& Narasimhan (1975) and Desale \& Ramanan (1975). In this section we shall compare those methods with ours.

We begin with an example by spelling out in detail our results for the simplest interesting case, namely for $n = 2$ and $k = 1$. Our basic theorem 7.14 becomes
\[
\mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right) + \sum _ { r = 0 } ^ { \infty } t ^ { 2 ( 2 r + g ) } \mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { r } \right) = \mathscr { G } P _ { t } ( \mathscr { C } )
\]
where $\mathscr { G } P _ { t }$ stands for $\mathscr { G }$-equivariant Poincaré series and $\mathscr { C } _ { r }$ is the stratum corresponding to unstable bundles of type $( r + 1 , - r )$ (i.e. of the form (11.10)). As shown in § 9 (see (9.3) and (9.7)), for the stable bundles we have
\[
\mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { s } } \right) = \frac { P _ { t } ( N ( 2,1 ) ) } { 1 - t ^ { 2 } } = \frac { ( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g } P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( 2,1 ) \right) } { 1 - t ^ { 2 } } .
\]
For the unstable stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { r }$ we apply (7.12) to see that
\[
\mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { r } \right) = \left\{ \frac { ( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g } } { 1 - t ^ { 2 } } \right\} ^ { 2 } .
\]
Finally for the whole space we apply theorem 2.15, which, for $n = 2$, gives
\[
\mathscr { G } P _ { t } ( \mathscr { C } ) = P _ { t } ( B \mathscr { G } ) = \frac { \left\{ ( 1 + t ) \left( 1 + t ^ { 3 } \right) \right\} ^ { 2 g } } { \left( 1 - t ^ { 2 } \right) ^ { 2 } \left( 1 - t ^ { 4 } \right) }
\]
Substituting these into (11.1) and cancelling a common factor $( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 } / \left( 1 - t ^ { 2 } \right)$, we get
\[
P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( 2,1 ) \right) + \frac { ( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g } } { 1 - t ^ { 2 } } \sum _ { r = 0 } ^ { \infty } t ^ { 2 ( 2 r + g ) } = \frac { \left( 1 + t ^ { 3 } \right) ^ { 2 g } } { \left( 1 - t ^ { 2 } \right) \left( 1 - t ^ { 4 } \right) } .
\]
Summing the geometric series we see that this gives the formula
\[
P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( 2,1 ) \right) = \frac { \left( 1 + t ^ { 3 } \right) ^ { 2 g } - t ^ { 2 g } ( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g } } { \left( 1 - t ^ { 2 } \right) \left( 1 - t ^ { 4 } \right) } .
\]

It is true, though not entirely transparent, that this rational function is in fact a polynomial in $t$ with non-negative integer coefficients (giving the Betti numbers). Moreover
\[
\operatorname { dim } N _ { 0 } ( 2,1 ) = 6 g - 6
\]
and so Poincaré duality requires that
\[
P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } \right) = t ^ { 6 g - 6 } P _ { 1 / t } \left( N _ { 0 } \right) .
\]

We turn now to summarize the methods of Harder \& Narasimhan.
We begin by taking a curve $M$ of genus $g$ defined over a finite field $F _ { q }$. The $\zeta$-function of $M$ then has the form
\[
\zeta _ { M } ( s ) = \left\{ \prod _ { i = 1 } ^ { 2 g } \left( 1 - \omega _ { i } q ^ { - s } \right) \right\} / \left( 1 - q ^ { - s } \right) \left( 1 - q \cdot q ^ { - s } \right) ,
\]
where the $\omega _ { i }$ are algebraic integers (depending on $M$ ) with $\left| \omega _ { i } \right| = q ^ { \frac { 1 } { 2 } }$. We now consider vector bundles $E$ over $M$ that are defined over $F _ { q }$ and have given rank $n$ and fixed determinant of degree $k$. This means that we fix the isomorphism class of the line-bundle $L = \operatorname { det } E$. Then the Siegel formula is the following:
\[
\sum _ { E } \frac { 1 } { | \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) | } = \frac { 1 } { q - 1 } q ^ { \left( n ^ { 2 } - 1 \right) ( q - 1 ) } \zeta _ { M } ( 2 ) \ldots \zeta _ { M } ( n ) ,
\]
where the sum is over all isomorphism classes (with $\operatorname { det } E$ fixed), and $| \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) |$ is the number of automorphisms of $E$.

Thus (11.6) counts the number of isomorphism classes of $E$, each being weighted inversely by its number of automorphisms. In particular stable bundles that admit only scalar automorphisms occur with weight $( q - 1 ) ^ { - 1 }$ and so contribute
\[
\left| N _ { L } ( n , k ) \right| / ( q - 1 )
\]
to the sum in (11.6), where the numerator denotes the number of classes of stable bundles of rank $n$ and determinant $L$ (of degree $k$ ) defined over $F _ { q }$. Now when $( n , k ) = 1$ the moduli space $N _ { L } ( n , k )$ of stable bundles of rank $n$ and determinant $L$ is a projective non-singular variety and we can suppose that it is also defined over $F _ { q }$ (if not replace $F _ { q }$ by a finite extension). Then, as the notation suggests, the numerator in (11.7) is just the number of points of the moduli space that are defined over $F _ { q }$. By the Weil conjectures, as established by Grothendieck and Deligne, the numbers of rational points over $F _ { q ^ { n } }$, for all $n$, determine the Betti numbers of the 'corresponding variety' over $C$. In our case this means the moduli space for stable bundles of fixed determinant over a Riemann surface of genus $g$ : the variety denoted in § 9 by $N _ { 0 } ( n , k )$.

In this way (11.6) will lead to a formula for the Poincaré polynomial $P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( n , k ) \right)$ provided we can deal with all the terms arising from unstable bundles. This can be done inductively, but for this purpose we need to consider also the non-coprime case and so we introduce
\[
\beta ( n , k ) = \Sigma \frac { 1 } { | \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) | } \quad \text { summed over semi-stable } E .
\]
Using the canonical filtration of Harder-Narasimhan explained in §7 we can collect together in (11.6) all terms of the same type. These can then be summed explicitly in terms of $\beta \left( n _ { i } , k _ { i } \right)$ and the number of rational points $J _ { q }$ on the Jacobian of $M$. Now in terms of the $\omega _ { i }$ occurring in (11.5) this is given by
\[
J _ { q } = \prod _ { i = 1 } ^ { 2 g } \left( 1 - \omega _ { i } \right) .
\]

Finally therefore (11.6) gives an explicit inductive formula for $\beta ( n , k )$ in terms of $\beta \left( n _ { i } , k _ { i } \right)$ with $n _ { i } < n$. The formula is given rationally in $q$ and the $\omega _ { i }$ and is independent of the line-bundle $L$ (Desale \& Ramanan 1975; proposition 1.7). To get the Poincaré polynomial $P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( n , k ) \right)$ one now makes the substitution
\[
\omega _ { i } \rightarrow - t , q \rightarrow t ^ { 2 } .
\]

For the purposes of comparison with our method let us now examine in detail the case $n = 2$ and $k = 1$. In (11.6) we then have stable bundles, which contribute (11.7), and unstable bundles, which have a canonical filtration
\[
0 \rightarrow L _ { 1 } \rightarrow E \rightarrow L _ { 2 } \rightarrow 0
\]
where $\operatorname { deg } L _ { 1 } = r + 1 , \operatorname { deg } L _ { 2 } = - r$ for $r = 0,1 , \ldots$ and $L _ { 1 } \otimes L _ { 2 } \cong L$. To compute $| \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) |$ consider separately the trivial and non-trivial extensions. For $E = L _ { 1 } \oplus L _ { 2 }$ the automorphisms consist of $F _ { q } ^ { * } \times F _ { q } ^ { * }$ together with the unipotents of the form $1 + \phi$ with
\[
\phi \in \operatorname { Hom } \left( L _ { 2 } , L _ { 1 } \right) = H ^ { 0 } \left( M , L _ { 2 } ^ { * } \otimes L _ { 1 } \right) .
\]
Hence
\[
| \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) | = ( q - 1 ) ^ { 2 } h _ { 0 } ,
\]
where $h _ { 0 } = \left| H ^ { 0 } \left( M , L _ { 2 } ^ { * } \otimes L _ { 1 } \right) \right|$. On the other hand for non-trivial extensions we have only one copy of $F _ { q } ^ { * }$ and so
\[
| \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) | = ( q - 1 ) h _ { 0 } .
\]
The non-trivial extensions correspond to non-zero elements of $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L _ { 2 } ^ { * } \otimes L _ { 1 } \right)$ and proportional vectors give isomorphic bundles. Hence the number of isomorphism classes of bundles $E$ for which (11.10) is non-trivial is
\[
\left( h _ { 1 } - 1 \right) / ( q - 1 ) ,
\]
where
\[
h _ { 1 } = \left| H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L _ { 2 } ^ { * } \otimes L _ { 1 } \right) \right| .
\]

Hence the contribution to the sum in (11.6) arising from a given $L _ { 1 }$ ( $L _ { 2 }$ being then determined as $\left. L \otimes L _ { 1 } ^ { * } \right)$ is
\[
\frac { 1 } { ( q - 1 ) ^ { 2 } h _ { 0 } } + \frac { h _ { 1 } - 1 } { ( q - 1 ) ^ { 2 } h _ { 0 } } = \frac { h _ { 1 } } { ( q - 1 ) ^ { 2 } h _ { 0 } } .
\]
Now by Riemann-Roch we have
\[
\operatorname { dim } H ^ { 0 } \left( M , L _ { 2 } ^ { * } \otimes L _ { 1 } \right) - \operatorname { dim } H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L _ { 2 } ^ { * } \otimes L _ { 1 } \right) = 2 r + 2 - g ,
\]
and so
\[
h _ { 0 } / h _ { 1 } = q ^ { 2 r + 2 - g } .
\]
Thus (11.6) becomes
\[
\frac { \left| N _ { L } ( 2,1 ) \right| } { q - 1 } + \frac { J _ { q } } { ( q - 1 ) ^ { 2 } } \sum _ { r = 0 } ^ { \infty } \frac { 1 } { q ^ { 2 r + 2 - g } } = \frac { 1 } { q - 1 } q ^ { 3 g - 3 } \zeta _ { M } ( 2 ) .
\]
Substituting for $\zeta _ { M } ( 2 )$ from (11.5) and for $J _ { q }$ from (11.9) we get
\[
\left| N _ { L } ( 2,1 ) \right| + \frac { q ^ { g - 2 } \prod _ { i = 1 } ^ { 2 g } \left( 1 - \omega _ { i } \right) } { ( q - 1 ) \left( 1 - q ^ { - 2 } \right) } = \frac { q ^ { 3 g - 3 } \prod _ { i = 1 } ^ { 2 g } \left( 1 - \omega _ { i } q ^ { - 2 } \right) } { \left( 1 - q ^ { - 2 } \right) \left( 1 - q ^ { - 1 } \right) } .
\]
If we now make the substitution $\omega _ { i } \rightarrow - t , q \rightarrow t ^ { 2 }$ then the expression for $\left| N _ { L } ( 2,1 ) \right|$ given by (11.12) converts into the formula for the Poincaré polynomial given in (11.3).

Comparing these two derivations of the formula for $P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( 2,1 ) \right)$, we see that they are formally
very similar with (11.11) playing the role of (11.2). We note, however, that (11.11) involves a convergent power series in $q ^ { - 1 }$ while (11.2) involves a formal power series in $t$. This makes it surprising that we should have made the substitution $q \rightarrow t ^ { 2 }$. In fact it is better to make the substitution
\[
q \rightarrow t ^ { - 2 } , \quad \omega _ { i } \rightarrow - t ^ { - 1 } .
\]
In view of the Poincaré duality formula (11.4) we must now get
\[
\left| N _ { L } ( 2,1 ) \right| \rightarrow t ^ { 6 - 6 g } P _ { t } \left( N _ { 0 } ( 2,1 ) \right) .
\]
Making the substitution (11.13) in (11.11) we see that, after removing the factor $( q - 1 ) ^ { - 1 }$ and multiplying by $q ^ { 6 g - 6 }$, we get precisely (11.2), with a correspondence term by term.

If we were to compute for $n = 2$ and $k = 0$, we could still compare (11.6) with (7.14) but we would not be dealing with the moduli space. Thus the leading term in (11.6) is the quantity $\beta ( 2,0 )$ defined in (11.8), while in (7.14) it would be the equivariant Poincaré series of the semistable stratum. Instead of (11.11) and (11.1) we then get
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \beta ( 2,0 ) + \frac { J _ { q } } { ( q - 1 ) ^ { 2 } } \sum _ { r = 1 } ^ { \infty } \frac { 1 } { q ^ { 2 r + 1 - g } } = \frac { q ^ { 3 g - 3 } } { q - 1 } \zeta _ { M } ( 2 ) , \\
& \mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) + \sum _ { r = 1 } ^ { \infty } t ^ { 2 ( 2 r + g - 1 ) } \mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { r } \right) = \mathscr { G } P _ { t } ( \mathscr { C } ) ,
\end{aligned}
\]
where the stratum $\mathscr { C } _ { r }$ corresponds now to extensions (11.10) with $\operatorname { deg } L _ { 1 } = r , \operatorname { deg } L _ { 2 } = - r$. Comparing these two formulae we see that the substitution (11.13) now leads to
\[
\beta ( 2,0 ) \rightarrow \frac { t ^ { 6 - 6 g } } { ( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g } } \mathscr { G } P _ { t } \left( \mathscr { C } _ { \mathrm { ss } } \right) ,
\]
the denominator $( 1 + t ) ^ { 2 g }$ arising only because on the right we did not fix the determinant.
We see therefore that, by making $\beta ( n , k )$ in general correspond to the equivariant Poincaré series of the semi-stable stratum, (11.6) and (7.14) lead to identical inductive procedures. It remains now to explain the origin of (11.6) and its relation to (7.14).

Just as the Jacobian arises classically as the group of divisor classes so moduli spaces of vector bundles can be viewed in terms of 'matrix divisor classes' as originally described by Weil (1938). In modern terminology this is best formulated in the language of adèles. Thus let $K$ be the function field of $M$ over $F _ { q }$ and for any affine algebraic group $G$ let $G _ { A }$ be the adèle group of $K$, i.e. the restricted product of $G _ { K _ { p } }$ where $p$ runs over all valuations of $K$, and $K _ { p }$ is the corresponding local field. Then $G _ { A }$ is a locally compact topological group and $G _ { K }$ is a discrete subgroup. For $G = G L _ { n }$, the isomorphism classes of vector bundles of rank $n$ over $M$ (defined over $F _ { q }$ ) are in bijective correspondence with the double coset space
\[
\mathfrak { K } \backslash G _ { A } / G _ { K } ,
\]
where $\mathfrak { K }$ is a maximal compact subgroup of $G _ { A }$. To understand this correspondence one should think of $\Re \backslash G _ { A }$ as a (multiplicative) matrix divisor and dividing by $G _ { K }$ as rational equivalence. In terms of bundles it corresponds to describing a bundle by a basis of meromorphic sections. If we take $G = S L _ { n }$ there are different maximal compact subgroups $\Re$ for different choices of the line bundle $L = \operatorname { det } E$, and the corresponding double cosets are in bijective correspondence with classes of $L$-oriented bundles, i.e. bundles $E$ together with a chosen isomorphism $\operatorname { det } E \cong L$. Now on $G _ { \boldsymbol { A } }$ one introduces a special choice of Haar measure, the Tamagawa measure $\boldsymbol { \tau }$. The total
measure of $G _ { A } / G _ { K }$ is finite and is called the Tamagawa number. For $S L _ { n }$ it turns out to be 1. Decomposing $G _ { A } / G _ { K }$ into $\mathfrak { K }$-orbits then leads to the formula
\[
\sum _ { \alpha } \tau \left( \mathfrak { A } / \mathfrak { N } _ { \alpha } \right) = \tau \left( G _ { A } / G _ { K } \right) = 1 ,
\]
where $\alpha$ runs over the orbits and $\mathfrak { K } _ { \alpha }$ is the (finite) isotropy group of the orbit. Dividing by $\tau ( \mathfrak { K } )$ then gives
\[
\sum _ { \alpha } \frac { 1 } { \left| \hat { \mathfrak { K } } _ { \alpha } \right| } = \frac { 1 } { \tau ( \mathfrak { K } ) } .
\]
If we denote by $| E |$ the number of inequivalent orientations on $E$, then for each orientation $\alpha$ on $E$ one has
\[
\frac { | \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) | } { \left| K _ { \alpha } \right| } = \frac { \left| F _ { \alpha } ^ { * } \right| } { | E | } = \frac { q - 1 } { | E | } .
\]
Hence if we rewrite (11.18) as a sum over isomorphism classes of vector bundles $E$ with fixed determinant, by ignoring the orientation, we get
\[
( q - 1 ) \sum _ { E } \frac { 1 } { | \operatorname { Aut } ( E ) | } = \frac { 1 } { \tau ( \mathfrak { K } ) } .
\]
This is the same as (11.6) in view of the formula
\[
\tau ( \mathfrak { K } ) ^ { - 1 } = q ^ { \left( n ^ { 2 } - 1 \right) ( g - 1 ) } \zeta _ { M } ( 2 ) \ldots \zeta _ { M } ( n ) .
\]
The factor $q - 1 = \left| F _ { q } ^ { * } \right|$ has arisen because of the passage from $G L _ { n }$ to $S L _ { n }$.
In comparing the derivation of (11.6) and (7.14) we see that in both cases we start from an infinite-dimensional space that describes all bundles, but in a redundant fashion. In one case this space is $G _ { A } / G _ { K }$ while in the other it is the space $\mathscr { C }$. As already noted the first description of algebraic bundles relies on the fact that every bundle is trivial over $K$, i.e. that it has a basis of rational or meromorphic sections. In the Riemann surface case we used instead the fact that all holomorphic bundles with the same degree (or Chern class) are differentiably equivalent.

In both cases we now stratify this infinite-dimensional space according to the type of the bundle, so that we have a unique open stratum given by semi-stable bundles. Moreover we have a group acting, preserving the strata, so that the equivalence classes represent isomorphism classes of bundles. In one case the group is $\mathfrak { K }$, the maximal compact subgroup of $G _ { A }$, while in the other it is the group of $C ^ { \infty }$ complex automorphisms. These equivariant stratifications can now be used to compute appropriate invariants. In the number-theory situation we compute Tamagawa measures to get (11.6) while in the geometric situation we compute equivariant cohomology to get (7.14). The parallel between these two procedures should be viewed as similar to that involved in the elementary computation with $P _ { n } ( C )$ in the Introduction. There are two notable differences here. In the first place the spaces concerned are infinite-dimensional and in the second place we work with equivariant notions relative to the appropriate group.

In these parallel treatments we see that in both cases the infinite-dimensional space itself is, in the appropriate sense, trivial. Thus the space $\mathscr { C }$ is contractible so that its ordinary Poincaré series is identically 1 , while the Tamagawa number of $G _ { A } / G _ { K }$ is also equal to 1 (notably it is independent of $q$ ). The next step is to 'divide' in the appropriate sense by the group action and to compute the result globally and locally and equate. On the global level we see therefore that the equivariant Poincaré series of $\mathscr { C }$, which is the same as the ordinary Poincaré series of $B \mathscr { G }$ and was computed in (2.15), corresponds to the measure $\tau ( \mathfrak { K } ) ^ { - 1 }$ given by (11.19). Using the explicit
formula (11.5) for $\zeta _ { M } ( s )$ and applying the substitution $\omega _ { i } \rightarrow - t ^ { - 1 } , q \rightarrow t ^ { - 2 }$ we see that except for trivial factors corresponding to the difference between $S L _ { n }$ and $G L _ { n }$ and a power of $t$ (related to the dimension of the moduli space)
\[
\tau ( \mathfrak { K } ) ^ { - 1 } \rightarrow P _ { t } ( B \mathscr { G } ) .
\]
Thus the 'global' terms in (11.6) and (7.14) correspond. On the other hand each stratum or type $\mu$ produces a 'local' term in both cases and when due account is taken of the isotropy groups the resulting formulae in the two cases again correspond. Thus the 'weighted counting' process corresponds to the use of equivariant cohomology.

When we compare these two basic methods of computing Betti numbers, i.e. the number-theory method and the Morse-theory method, we see that in each case we need to be fortunate to get an explicit answer. Thus when counting up points with a stratification the answer is clearly additive but in general we may not know how to compute the number of points in each stratum. In the Morse theory method each stratum retracts onto its critical set but we have no guarantee that the exact sequences split, i.e. that we have a perfect Morse stratification. In our present case the reason why we can count points effectively is that each stratum is made up of affine spaces corresponding to extensions as illustrated above. On the topological side the perfect nature of the stratification arises from the isotropy group behaviour. This is presumably linked in some way with the affine space decomposition of the strata.

Another reason that sometimes simplifies the process of counting points is if all homology is represented by algebraic cycles. In that case Frobenius acts on $H ^ { 2 r }$ by $q ^ { r }$ and so there are no mysterious eigenvalues. In our case this is nearly true in the sense that all rational cohomology of the moduli space $N$ or $N _ { 0 }$ is generated, as shown in § 9, by the Künneth components of the Chern classes of the universal bundle on $M \times N$. Thus the only eigenvalues other than powers of $q$ arise from $H ^ { 1 } ( M )$ and these are the $\omega _ { i }$ that appeared above. This explains why the simple substitution $\omega _ { i } \rightarrow - t ^ { - 1 } , q \rightarrow t ^ { - 2 }$ is all that is required to convert the number-theory formulae into Poicaré series formulae.

Now that we have described the detailed correspondence between our method and that of Harder-Narasimhan many questions arise. In the first place why is the Tamagawa number of $S L _ { n }$ equal to 1 ? This is not very well understood but analogy with our method suggests that it might have some cohomological significance. Why moreover do we have the remarkable correspondence (11.20) and the analogy exhibited in (11.6) and (2.9), between the separate factors of both sides, namely $\zeta _ { M } ( k )$ and $P _ { t } ( \operatorname { Map } ( M , K ( Z , 2 k ) )$ ? This and other aspects of the comparison suggest that the basic relation between numbers of points and Betti numbers for algebraic varieties may have some extension to infinite dimensions in which counting of points is replaced by a suitable measure.

Speculating in another direction we recall that the Yang-Mills equations arise in physics and that to quantize them involves, at least heuristically, some process of integration over function spaces. Comparison with the number theory suggests that there might be a natural measure, depending perhaps on some real parameter $t$, so that what we have been computing as Poincaré series actually turn out to be measures.

\section*{12. Convexity and Lie groups}

This section is essentially an appendix concerned with the partial ordering that we have encountered in our stratification of the space $\mathscr { C }$. We shall take this opportunity of giving a brief but essentially self-contained account, which emphasizes the role of convexity in Lie groups. The results are not essentially new, and can mainly be found in Horn (1954) for the unitary case and in Kostant (1973) for the general groups, but our presentation brings out those aspects that are of particular relevance to the theory of bundles and connections. In particular we stress the role of convex invariant functions on the Lie algebra. For an extensive account of some aspects see also Marshall \& Olkin (1979).

For simplicity we shall begin with the partial ordering (7.7) for sequences $\left( \lambda _ { 1 } , \ldots , \lambda _ { n } \right)$ of real numbers. Thus one defines $\mu \leqslant \lambda$ if, after arranging each sequence in decreasing order, we have
\[
\left\{ \begin{array} { l } 
\sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { i } \mu _ { j } \leqslant \sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { i } \lambda _ { j } \quad \text { for } \quad i = 1 , \ldots , n - 1 , \\
\sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { n } \mu _ { j } = \sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { n } \lambda _ { j } .
\end{array} \right.
\]
This partial ordering occurs in Horn (1954) where it is shown to be equivalent to either of the following properties:
\[
\begin{gathered}
\sum _ { j } f \left( \mu _ { j } \right) \leqslant \sum _ { j } f \left( \lambda _ { j } \right) \quad \text { for every convex function } f : R \rightarrow R ; \\
\mu = P \lambda \text { where } \lambda , \mu \in R ^ { n } \text { and } P \text { is a doubly stochastic matrix. }
\end{gathered}
\]
We recall that a real square matrix $P = \left( p _ { i j } \right)$ is stochastic if $p _ { i j } \geqslant 0$ and $\sum _ { j } p _ { i j } = 1$ for all $i$. If in addition the transposed matrix is also stochastic then $P$ is called doubly stochastic. A theorem of G. D. Birkhoff identifies doubly stochastic matrices in terms of permutation matrices, namely

The doubly stochastic $n \times n$ matrices are the convex hull of the permutation matrices.
In view of this (12.3) can be replaced by
\[
\widehat { \Sigma _ { n } \mu } \subset \widehat { \Sigma _ { n } \lambda }
\]
where $\Sigma _ { n } x$ denotes the orbit of any $x \in R ^ { n }$ under the permutation group $\Sigma _ { n }$, and $\hat { C }$ denotes the convex hull of the set $C \subset R ^ { n }$.

Geometric notions of convexity can be dualized into statements about convex functions by virtue of the fact that, for $C \subset R ^ { n }$,
\[
x \in \hat { C } \Leftrightarrow \phi ( x ) \leqslant \sup _ { C } \phi \quad \text { for all convex } \quad \phi : R ^ { n } \rightarrow R .
\]
Thus taking $\phi$ to be a convex symmetric function on $R ^ { n }$ (i.e. invariant under $\Sigma _ { n }$ ) we can see that (12.4) implies
\[
\phi ( \mu ) \leqslant \phi ( \lambda ) \text { for all convex symmetric functions on } R ^ { n } \text {. }
\]
Since (12.2) is the special case of (12.5) for functions $\phi \left( x _ { 1 } , \ldots , x _ { n } \right)$ of the form $\sum _ { i = 1 } ^ { n } f \left( x _ { i } \right)$, it follows that (12.5) implies (12.2) and so is equivalent to all the other properties.

Schur showed that if $\mu _ { j } ( j = 1 , \ldots , n )$ are the diagonal elements of a hermitian matrix whose eigenvalues are $\lambda _ { j }$, then $\mu \leqslant \lambda$ in the sense of (12.1). Horn (1954) proved the converse so that another equivalent of (12.1) is
\[
\text { the } \lambda _ { j } \text { are the eigenvalues of a hermitian matrix with diagonal elements } \mu _ { j } \text {. }
\]

A hermitian matrix $A$ is determined, up to conjugacy by $U ( n )$, by the unordered set of its eigenvalues, or equivalently by the orbit $\Sigma _ { n } \lambda \in R ^ { n }$. If $\Sigma _ { n } \mu$ corresponds to the conjugacy class $C ( B )$ of a hermitian matrix $B$ then (12.4) clearly implies that $C ( B )$ lies in the convex hull of $C ( A )$. Conversely if a diagonal matrix $B$, with eigenvalues $\mu _ { j }$, lies in the convex hull of $C ( A )$ it must lie in the convex hull of the diagonal parts of the matrices in $C ( A )$. But by Schur's result this means that $\mu \in R ^ { n }$ is in the convex hull of $\Sigma _ { n } \lambda$, where the $\lambda _ { j }$ are the eigenvalues of $A$. Hence (12.1) is also equivalent to
\[
\widehat { C ( \mu ) } \subset \widehat { C ( \lambda ) } ,
\]
where $C ( \lambda )$ denotes the conjugacy class of hermitian matrices with the given eigenvalues $\lambda _ { j }$.
As before (12.7) implies
\[
\psi ( B ) \leqslant \psi ( A ) \text { for all convex invariant functions } \psi \text { on the space of hermitian matrices, }
\]
where $B \in C ( \mu )$ and $A \in C ( \lambda )$. Clearly such a convex invariant $\psi$ defines a convex symmetric function $\phi$ on $R ^ { n }$ by putting $\psi ( A ) = \phi ( \lambda )$. Thus (12.8) is also directly implied by (12.5). The converse is not quite so clear because it is by no means obvious that convexity of $\phi$ on $R ^ { n }$ implies convexity of $\psi$ on the space of hermitian matrices. We shall, however, prove that this is in fact true, so that (12.8) is equivalent to all the earlier properties. This proof is just as easily given in the more general context of a general compact Lie group, so we move on now to consider how one generalizes all the preceding ideas.

For a general compact Lie group $G$, the role of the hermitian (or rather skew-hermitian) matrices is played now by the Lie algebra $\mathfrak { g }$ of $G$. The diagonal matrices are replaced by the Lie algebra $\mathfrak { t }$ of a maximal torus $T$ of $G$ and $\Sigma _ { n }$ becomes the Weyl group $W$. Writing a set of $\lambda _ { j }$ in decreasing order corresponds to picking a (closed) positive Weyl chamber $C$ in $\mathfrak { t }$ : this is a fundamental domain for the action of $W$. If we fix once and for all a bi-invariant inner product on $\mathfrak { g }$ we get a $W$-invariant inner product on $\mathfrak { t }$ and we define $C ^ { * }$ to be the dual cone of $C$, namely
\[
x \in C ^ { * } \Leftrightarrow \langle x , y \rangle \geqslant 0 \text { for all } y \in C .
\]
In the semi-simple case $C$ and $C ^ { * }$ are both of maximal dimension but if $\mathfrak { g }$ has a non-trivial centre then $C ^ { * }$ lies in the subspace orthogonal to the centre, i.e. in the semi-simple part. The following lemma relating $W , C$ and $C ^ { * }$ is then standard (Bourbaki 1968, ch. VI, prop. 18).

Lemma 12.10. $x \in C \Leftrightarrow ( 1 - \omega ) x \in C ^ { * }$ for all $\omega \in W$.
The cone $C ^ { * }$ defines a natural partial ordering on $\mathfrak { t }$ by
\[
x \geqslant y \Leftrightarrow x - y \in C ^ { * } .
\]
With this notation (12.10) can be rewritten as
\[
x \in C \Leftrightarrow x \geqslant \omega x \text { for all } \omega \in W .
\]

For $U ( n )$ the cone $C$ as already mentioned is given by the conditions $x _ { 1 } \geqslant x _ { 2 } \geqslant \ldots \geqslant x _ { n }$, the standard inner product $\langle x , y \rangle = \sum x _ { i } y _ { i }$ can be rewritten as
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \langle x , y \rangle = \left( x _ { 1 } - x _ { 2 } \right) y _ { 1 } + \left( x _ { 2 } - x _ { 3 } \right) \left( y _ { 1 } + y _ { 2 } \right) + \ldots \\
& + \quad + \left( x _ { n - 1 } - x _ { n } \right) \left( y _ { 1 } + \ldots + y _ { n - 1 } \right) + x _ { n } \left( y _ { 1 } + \ldots + y _ { n } \right)
\end{aligned}
\]
showing that the cone $C ^ { * }$ is given by
\[
\sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { i } y _ { j } \geqslant 0 \quad \text { for } \quad 1 \leqslant i \leqslant n - 1
\]
and
\[
\sum _ { 1 } ^ { n } y _ { j } = 0 .
\]
Thus the partial ordering (12.11) reduces in this case to that given by (12.1).
The following lemma, which is an easy corollary of (12.10), generalizes the equivalence of (12.1) and (12.5).

Lemma 12.14. If $x , y \in C$ then
\[
y \in \widehat { W } x \Leftrightarrow y \leqslant x .
\]

Proof. Let us first illustrate the geometric meaning of this for $\operatorname { SU } ( 3 )$, in which case the diagram is as below.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/e9a39117-9581-4f5f-a81d-70aed9c3fba4-80.jpg?height=547&width=596&top_left_y=824&top_left_x=580}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Figure 4.}
\end{figure}
The shaded region is the intersection $C$ with the 'backward' $C ^ { * }$-cone centred at $x$. It therefore describes the set $y$ such that
\[
y \in C \quad \text { and } \quad y \leqslant x .
\]
The lemma asserts that this set is also the intersection of $C$ and the hexagon $\widehat { W x }$. In one direction this is easy because for $y \in \widehat { W } x$ we have
\[
y = \sum _ { \omega \in W } a _ { \omega } \omega x , \quad a _ { \omega } \geqslant 0 , \quad \sum a _ { \omega } = 1
\]
so that
\[
x = \left( \sum a _ { \omega } \right) x \geqslant \sum a _ { \omega } \omega x = y
\]
by (12.12). For the converse it will be enough by continuity to assume that $x$ is an interior point of $C$ and that $x - y$ is an interior point of $C ^ { * }$. The directed line $x y$ then meets the boundary of $C$ in a point $z$ and we must show that the whole finite interval of $x z$ lies in $\widehat { W x }$. Since the relation $y \in \widehat { W x }$ is transitive, it will be enough to show that there is a constant $c ( x , z )$ so that, if $y = t z + ( 1 - t ) x$, with $0 \leqslant t \leqslant 1$, is any point in the interval $z x$, then
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $c ( y , z ) \geqslant c ( x , z )$,
\item[(ii)] $y \in \widehat { W x }$ if $t \leqslant c ( x , z )$.
\end{itemize}
A finite number $N$ of repetitions, where $N ^ { - 1 } < c ( x , z )$, will then prove that the whole interval $z x \in \widehat { W } x$.

Now let $\alpha _ { i }$ be the simple roots normalized to have length one, so that the $\alpha _ { i }$ are the unit normals to the faces of $C$ and form the basis for $C ^ { * }$. Since $x$ is assumed interior to $C$ we have
\[
\left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle > 0 \text { for all } i .
\]
Since $x - z$ is assumed interior to $C ^ { * }$ we have
\[
x - z = \sum _ { i = 1 } ^ { l } a _ { i } \alpha _ { i } , \quad a _ { i } > 0 .
\]
Now let $\omega _ { i } \in W$ be the reflexion in the face $\left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle = 0$ so that
\[
\omega _ { i } x = x - 2 \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle \alpha _ { i } ,
\]
and define for $0 \leqslant t \leqslant 1$ constants
\[
b _ { i } = t a _ { i } / 2 \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle , \quad b = 1 - \sum b _ { i } .
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
b x + \sum b _ { i } \omega _ { i } x & = x + \sum b _ { i } \left( \omega _ { i } x - x \right) = x - 2 \sum b _ { i } \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle \alpha _ { i } \\
& = x - t \sum a _ { i } \alpha _ { i } = t z + ( 1 - t ) x = y .
\end{aligned}
\]
Hence $y \in \widehat { W } x$ provided $b _ { i } \geqslant 0$ and $b \geqslant 0$, and this will hold if
\[
0 \leqslant t \leqslant 2 \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle / l a _ { i } \text { for all } i .
\]
It remains to examine the quantity
\[
c _ { i } ( x , z ) = 2 \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle / l a _ { i } ,
\]
when we vary $x$ on the interval $z x$. Replacing $x$ by the variable point $y = t z + ( 1 - t ) x , a _ { i }$ gets replaced by $( 1 - t ) a _ { i }$ and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\left\langle y , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle & = t \left\langle z , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle + ( 1 - t ) \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle \\
& \geqslant ( 1 - t ) \left\langle x , \alpha _ { i } \right\rangle \text { since } z \in C \quad \text { and } \quad t \geqslant 0 .
\end{aligned}
\]
Hence $c _ { i } ( y , z ) \geqslant c _ { i } ( x , z )$ and the proof is completed by taking $c ( x , z ) = \min _ { i } c _ { i } ( x , z )$.
Remark. The partial ordering $y \leqslant x$ for $x \in C$ is the usual ordering for dominant weights of representations, when we consider not the Lie algebra of $T$ but its dual. The reinterpretation in terms of convex hulls of $W$-orbits is given in Adams (1969). In our case we are interested not in representations but in conjugacy classes but the partial ordering is essentially the same.

Kostant (1973) proved the following generalization of the Schur-Horn theorem:
\[
\pi ( G y ) = \widehat { W } y
\]
where $y \in \mathfrak { t } , \pi : \mathfrak { g } \rightarrow \mathfrak { t }$ is orthogonal projection and $G y$ denotes the $G$-orbit of $y$ under the adjoint action. See also Atiyah (1982) for a different proof in a more general context. Using (12.15), or rather the easier half that gives the inclusion $\pi ( G y ) \subset W y$, we shall now prove the promised result about convex invariant functions:

Proposition 12.16. Let $\phi$ be a $W$-invariant convex function on t and $\psi$ the corresponding $G$-invariant function on $\mathfrak { g }$. Then $\psi$ is also convex.

Proof. For any function on $R ^ { n }$ it will be convenient to define $\Gamma ( f )$ to be the region 'above its graph', i.e. all points $( x , y )$ with $x \in R ^ { n } , y \in R$ such that $y > f ^ { \prime } ( x )$. Convexity of the function $f$ is then equivalent to convexity of $\Gamma ( f )$. Recall also that $\Gamma$ is convex if every boundary point $a$ has a supporting hyperplane $H _ { a }$ (i.e. $\Gamma$ is contained in one of the two half-spaces complementary to $H _ { a }$ ). Now consider the functions $\phi , \psi$ and the corresponding regions
\[
\Gamma ( \phi ) \subset \mathfrak { t } \oplus R , \quad \Gamma ( \psi ) \subset \mathfrak { g } \oplus R .
\]

Because $\psi$ is $G$-invariant so is $\Gamma ( \psi )$ and it is therefore sufficient to prove the existence of a supporting hyperplane to $\Gamma ( \psi )$ at boundary points $( \lambda , y )$ of $\Gamma ( \phi )$. By hypothesis $\Gamma ( \phi )$ is convex so we have a supporting hyperplane $H \subset \mathfrak { t } \oplus R$. Let $H ^ { \prime } = \pi ^ { - 1 } ( H ) \subset \mathfrak { g } \oplus R$ where $\pi$ is orthogonal projection. We shall show that $H ^ { \prime }$ is the required supporting hyperplane for $\Gamma ( \psi )$. Any point $( x , y ) \in \Gamma ( \psi )$ satisfies $y > \psi ( x )$. From (12.15) and the convexity of $\phi$ we see that
\[
\psi ( x ) \geqslant \phi ( \pi x ) .
\]
Hence $y > \phi ( \pi x )$ so that $( \pi x , y ) \in \Gamma ( \phi )$ and hence is on one side of $H$. This means precisely that $( x , y )$ is on the corresponding side of $H ^ { \prime }$, which completes the proof.

As with $U ( n )$ we can give several further equivalent definitions of the partial ordering and we summarize this in

Proposition 12.17. The following conditions $x , y \in \mathfrak { t }$ are all equivalent:
\[
\widehat { W y } \subset \widehat { W x } ;
\]
\[
\phi ( y ) \leqslant \phi ( x ) \text { for all } W \text {-invariant convex functions } \phi \text { on } \mathfrak { t } \text {; }
\]
\[
\widehat { G y } \subset \widehat { G x } ;
\]
\[
\psi ( y ) \leqslant \psi ( x ) \text { for all } G \text {-invariant convex functions } \psi \text { on } \mathfrak { g } \text {. }
\]

Proof. (1) ⇒ (2), (2) ⇒ (4), (1) $= ( 3 )$ and (3) ⇒ (4) are all trivial. (3) ⇒ (1) follows from (12.15) and (4) ⇒ (2) follows from (12.16). It remains to see that (2) ⇒ (1). For this we take
\[
\phi ( x ) = \sum _ { \omega \in W } \exp \left\langle \omega t x , e _ { i } \right\rangle \quad ( t > 0 ) ,
\]
where the $e _ { i }$ are a basis of $C$ (the 'edges' of the cone) and let $x , y \in C$. In view of (12.11) and (12.12) we have
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \left\langle x , e _ { i } \right\rangle \geqslant \left\langle \omega x , e _ { i } \right\rangle \quad \text { for } \quad \omega \neq 1 \quad \text { and all } \quad i , \\
& \left\langle y , e _ { i } \right\rangle \geqslant \left\langle \omega y , e _ { i } \right\rangle .
\end{aligned}
\]
It will be sufficient by continuity to suppose that both $x$ and $y$ are interior to $C$; then the above inequalities are strict so that for large $t$ the first term (for $\omega = 1$ ) in the sum defining $\phi$ is dominant. Hence
\[
\begin{aligned}
\phi ( y ) \leqslant \phi ( x ) & \Rightarrow \left\langle x , e _ { i } \right\rangle \geqslant \left\langle y , e _ { i } \right\rangle \text { for all } i \\
& \Rightarrow x - y \in C ^ { * } \\
& \Rightarrow y \leqslant x \text { by }
\end{aligned}
\]

Remark. Proposition 12.17 remains true when 'convex' is interpreted as 'smooth convex' (or even analytic). This is clear from the proof because, for the essential implication (2) ⇒ (1), we use only exponential functions.

If we take any irreducible representation $\rho : G \rightarrow U ( n )$ it has weights $\lambda _ { 1 } , \ldots , \lambda _ { n }$, which we may view as elements of $\mathfrak { t }$, so that any $x \in \mathfrak { t }$ gives rise to the hermitian matrix with eigenvalues $x _ { j } = \left\langle x , \lambda _ { j } \right\rangle$. If $\lambda _ { 1 }$ is the maximal weight then $\lambda _ { 1 } \in C$ and for all $j \neq 1 , \lambda _ { 1 } > \lambda _ { j }$, i.e. $\lambda _ { 1 } - \lambda _ { j } \in C ^ { * }$. This means that, if $x \in C$, then $x _ { 1 } \geqslant x _ { j }$ for $j \neq 1$, so that $x _ { 1 }$ is the largest eigenvalue. Hence if $x , y \in C$ and we assume $p ( y ) \leqslant \rho ( x )$ then in particular $y _ { 1 } \leqslant x _ { 1 }$, i.e. $\left\langle y , \lambda _ { 1 } \right\rangle \leqslant \left\langle x , \lambda _ { 1 } \right\rangle$. If we let $\rho$ run over all irreducible representations then $\lambda _ { 1 }$ runs over all integral dominant weights and these span $C$ : in fact there are $l$ basic integral weights $e _ { i }$ that lie in the edges of $C$ and generate it. This proves (cf. Kostant 1973)

Proposition 12.18. For $x , y \in \mathfrak { g }$ we have $y \leqslant x \Leftrightarrow \rho ( y ) \leqslant \rho ( x )$ for all unitary representations $\rho$.

This completes our survey of convexity and essentially contains all the results we have used earlier. Thus in §8 we used the inequality
\[
\phi \left[ \begin{array} { l l } 
A & B \\
C & D
\end{array} \right] \geqslant \phi \left[ \begin{array} { l l } 
\alpha & 0 \\
0 & \delta
\end{array} \right]
\]
for every convex invariant function $\phi$ on $\mathfrak { u } ( n )$, where $\left[ \begin{array} { l l } A & B \\ C & D \end{array} \right]$ is a skew-hermitian matrix in block form and $\alpha , \delta$ are the central components of $A , D$. This follows in fact from Horn's theorem and the observation that $\left[ \begin{array} { l l } \alpha & 0 \\ 0 & \delta \end{array} \right]$ is in the convex hull of the $\Sigma _ { n }$-orbit of the diagonal part of $\left[ \begin{array} { c c } A & B \\ C & D \end{array} \right]$. The same proof holds for the more general block decomposition also used in §8.

The result (8.21) (and its generalization to any $G$ ) is an easy consequence of (12.17). In fact if $\phi ( y ) = \phi ( x )$ for all $W$-invariant convex functions $\phi$ then $\widehat { W } y = \widehat { W } x$ and so the extreme points of these two convex polyhedra must coincide. But the extreme points of $\widehat { W } x$ are certainly among the finite set $W x$. Hence $W x$ and $W y$ intersect and so coincide.

\section*{13. Equivariant cohomology}

In this section we shall review some of the general facts about equivariant cohomology and establish some of the more particular results that we have had to use.

We recall first that for any topological group $G$ the classifying space $B G$ is defined as the base of a principal $G$-bundle whose total space is contractible. It is unique up to homotopy so that in particular $H ^ { * } ( B G )$ depends only on $G$. More generally if $G$ acts on a space $X$ we define $X _ { G }$ as the associated bundle over $B G$ with fibre $X$ and define the equivariant cohomology by
\[
H _ { G } ( X ) = H \left( X _ { G } \right) ,
\]
so that $H ( B G )$ is now the equivariant cohomology of a point. If $G$ acts freely on $X$ so that $X \rightarrow X / G$ is a principal $G$-bundle then the map $X _ { G } \rightarrow X / G$ has contractible fibres and so is a homotopy equivalence. Thus in this case
\[
H _ { G } ( X ) \cong H ( X / G ) .
\]

Suppose now that $K$ is a closed normal subgroup of $G$, and that $X$ is a $G$-space on which $K$ acts freely (with $X \rightarrow X / K$ a principal $K$-bundle). Then the quotient group $S = G / K$ acts on $Y = X / K$, and we have
\[
H _ { G } ( X ) \cong H _ { S } ( Y ) .
\]
To see this let $E _ { 1 } , E _ { 2 }$ be the total spaces of universal bundles of $G , S$ respectively. Note that $G$ acts on $E _ { 2 }$ via $S$ so that $E _ { 1 } \times E _ { 2 }$ is also a free contractible $G$-space; we can thus take
\[
X _ { G } = X \times _ { G } \left( E _ { 1 } \times E _ { 2 } \right) .
\]
Projecting onto $X \times { } _ { G } E _ { 2 }$ with fibre $E _ { 1 }$ is a homotopy equivalence and
\[
X \times _ { G } E _ { 2 } = Y \times _ { S } E _ { 2 } = Y _ { S }
\]
so that $X _ { G } \sim Y _ { S }$ proving (13.1).
So far we have been rather imprecise about the class of topological groups to be considered and the reader might feel uneasy about the use of these ideas for the large infinite-dimensional groups $\mathscr { G }$ of gauge transformations. In view of (13.1) we can effectively reduce all our application
of equivariant cohomology to the case when $G$ is a compact Lie group. In fact if $\mathscr { G }$ is the group of gauge transformations of a principal $G$-bundle over a manifold $M$, it has a normal subgroup $\mathscr { G } _ { 0 }$ consisting of transformations that are the identity at some fixed base point of $M$ and the quotient group is isomorphic to $G$. Moreover $\mathscr { G } _ { 0 }$ acts freely on the space of connections so that by (13.1)
\[
H _ { \mathscr { G } } ( \mathscr { A } ) \cong H _ { G } \left( \mathscr { A } / \mathscr { G } _ { 0 } \right)
\]
with a similar result for any $\mathscr { G }$-stable subspace of $\mathscr { A }$. We could therefore always work in $\mathscr { A } / \mathscr { G } _ { 0 }$ and use $G$-equivariant cohomology if we wished.

If $K \subset G$ and $Y$ is a $K$-space we define its 'extension' to a $G$-space $X$ by putting $X = G \times _ { K } Y$. Note that $X$ is just the bundle with fibre $Y$ and base $G / K$ associated with the principal $K$-bundle $G \rightarrow G / K$. If $E$ is a free contractible $G$-space then
\[
X _ { G } = E \times _ { G } X = E \times _ { G } G \times _ { K } Y = E \times _ { K } Y = Y _ { K }
\]
so that $H _ { G } ( X ) \cong H _ { K } ( Y )$.
We come now to some more specific results, which concern compact connected Lie groups $G$ without torsion in their cohomology. The examples we need are just $U ( n )$ and more generally products of the form $U \left( n _ { 1 } \right) \times \ldots \times U \left( n _ { r } \right)$. If $T$ is a maximal torus of $G$ then it is well known that the fibration
\[
G / T \rightarrow B _ { T } \rightarrow B _ { G }
\]
behaves like a product for integral cohomology and all the spaces involved have no torsion. It follows that, for any $G$-space $X$, the induced fibration
\[
G / T \rightarrow X _ { T } \rightarrow X _ { G }
\]
is multiplicative for integral cohomology
\[
H \left( X _ { T } \right) \cong H \left( X _ { G } \right) \otimes H ( G / T ) ,
\]
so that
\[
H _ { G } ( X ) \quad \text { is a direct summand of } \quad H _ { T } ( X ) .
\]
or equivalently for all primes $p$
\[
H _ { G } \left( X , Z _ { p } \right) \rightarrow H _ { T } \left( X , Z _ { p } \right) \quad \text { is injective } .
\]

Next let $T = T _ { 0 } \times T _ { 1 }$ be the product of two subtori with $T _ { 0 }$ acting trivially on the connected $T$-space $X$. Then
\[
X _ { T } = B T _ { 0 } \times X _ { T _ { 1 } }
\]
so that for $Z _ { p }$ coefficients (and any prime $p$ )
\[
H _ { T } ( X ) \cong H \left( B T _ { 0 } \right) \otimes H _ { T _ { 1 } } ( X ) .
\]
Now $H \left( B T _ { 0 } \right)$ is a polynomial ring and so any $\alpha _ { 0 } \in H \left( B _ { T 0 } \right)$ with $\alpha _ { 0 } \neq 0$ is not a zero-divisor in $H _ { T } ( X )$. More generally if $\alpha \in H _ { T } ( X )$ restricts to such an $\alpha _ { 0 }$, i.e. if
\[
\alpha = \alpha _ { 0 } \otimes 1 + \text { terms of positive degree in } H _ { T _ { 1 } } ( X ) ,
\]
the same holds. This follows on filtering by the degree in $H _ { T _ { 1 } } ( X )$ and noticing that $\alpha$ acts via $\alpha _ { 0 }$ on the associated graded module.

In our application the element $\alpha _ { 0 }$ above will occur as the Chern class of a vector bundle $N _ { T _ { 0 } }$ over $B T _ { 0 }$ arising from a representation $N$ of $T _ { 0 }$. For $\operatorname { dim } N = 1$ the assignment
\[
N \rightarrow c _ { 1 } \left( N _ { T _ { 0 } } \right)
\]
gives rise to an isomorphism
\[
\widehat { T } _ { 0 } \cong H ^ { 2 } \left( B T _ { 0 } , Z \right) ,
\]
(where $\hat { T } _ { 0 }$ is the character group of $T _ { 0 }$ ) which we shall consider as an identification. The whole cohomology ring $H ^ { * } \left( B T _ { 0 } , Z \right)$ can then be viewed as the symmetric algebra of the lattice $\widehat { T } _ { 0 }$. For an $n$-dimensional representation $N$ therefore we decompose
\[
N = \sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { n } L _ { j }
\]
into one-dimensional representations, and
\[
c _ { n } \left( N _ { T _ { 0 } } \right) = \prod _ { j = 1 } ^ { n } L _ { j } .
\]
If each $L _ { j }$ is primitive, i.e. is not divisible in $\widehat { T } _ { 0 }$ by any prime $p$, we shall say that $N$ is primitive. In this case $c _ { n } \left( N _ { T _ { 0 } } \right)$ is clearly non-zero when reduced $\bmod p$ for any $p$.

We shall now put all these remarks together into the following.
Proposition 13.4. Let $X$ be a connected $G$-space on which some subtorus $T _ { 0 }$ acts trivially and let $N$ be a $G$-vector bundle on $X$. Assume that the representation of $T _ { 0 }$ on the fibre of $N$ is primitive and that $H ( G )$ has no torsion. Then multiplication by the top Chern class $\alpha = c _ { n } \left( N _ { G } \right)$ on $H _ { G } \left( X , Z _ { p } \right)$ is injective for all primes $p$.

The proof follows from 13.3, which allows us to restrict from $G$ to a maximal torus $T \supset T _ { 0 }$, so that we are in the situation just discussed.

\section*{14. Sobolev spaces}

In this section we shall show, by introducing appropriate Sobolev spaces of functions, how to justify our heuristic use of infinite-dimensional manifolds. Much of this is standard and can be found in Narasimhan \& Ramadas (1979), Uhlenbeck (1981) or Mitter \& Viallet (1981) but some of the more detailed results related to the complex structure depend of course on the dimensionality of the base manifold being 2 . For this reason we shall give a self-contained account tailored to our purposes.

For the convenience of the reader we shall now recall some of the basic facts about Sobolev spaces. For fuller details we refer to Palais ( $1965 , \mathrm { ch } .9$ ). On a compact smooth $n$-dimensional manifold $M$ the space $L _ { k } ^ { p }$ (for $1 \leqslant p < \infty$ ) denotes those functions $f$ all of whose derivatives up to and including order $k$ are in the Lebesgue space $L ^ { p }$. The definition can be extended to nonintegral $k$ and to sections $f$ of any smooth complex vector bundle over $M$. Each $L _ { k } ^ { p }$ is a Banach space and for $p = 2$ is a Hilbert space also denoted by $H ^ { k }$. The Sobolev embedding theorems assert that
\[
\begin{array} { r } 
L _ { k } ^ { p } \subset L _ { l } ^ { q } \text { if } k \geqslant l \text { and } k - n / p \geqslant l - n / q \text { and the inclusion is compact } \\
\text { if we have strict inequalities, }
\end{array}
\]
\[
L _ { k } ^ { p } \subset C ^ { l } \quad \text { if } k - n / p > l , \quad \text { and the inclusion is compact. }
\]
Here $C ^ { l }$ (for integral $l \geqslant 0$ ) denotes as usual sections whose partial derivatives of order $\leqslant l$ are continuous. In particular
\[
\bigcap _ { k = 1 } ^ { \infty } H ^ { k } = C ^ { \infty } .
\]

Recall now the Hölder inequality
\[
\| f g \| _ { r } \leqslant \| f \| _ { p } \| g \| _ { q } ,
\]
where $\left\| \| _ { p } \right.$ denotes the $L ^ { p }$-norm and $r ^ { - 1 } = p ^ { - 1 } + q ^ { - 1 }$. This implies the continuity of the multiplication map
\[
L _ { 0 } ^ { p } \times L _ { 0 } ^ { q } \rightarrow L _ { 0 } ^ { r } .
\]
Applying this together with (14.1) one deduces
\[
L _ { k } ^ { p } \text { is a Banach algebra for } k > n / p \text { and } L _ { j } ^ { p } \text { is a topological } L _ { k } ^ { p } \text {-module for } 0 \leqslant j \leqslant k \text {. }
\]

In the good range $k > n / p$ one can also define nonlinear generalizations of the spaces $L _ { k } ^ { p }$. Thus one can define the space $L _ { k } ^ { p } ( M , N )$ of maps $f : M \rightarrow N$ where $N$ is another $C ^ { \infty }$ manifold. More generally one can define the $L _ { k } ^ { p }$-sections of a $C ^ { \infty }$ fibre bundle over $M$ with $N$ as fibre. These spaces are dense in the space of continuous sections. In particular on taking $N$ to be a Lie group and using (14.5) the $L _ { k } ^ { p }$ automorphisms of a vector bundle (or a unitary bundle) form Lie groups.

We come now to the special case that interests us, namely $n = \operatorname { dim } M = 2$. For a complex $C ^ { \infty }$ vector bundle $E$ over $M$ with hermitian metric we then have for $k \geqslant 2$ the real Lie group of unitary automorphisms of class $H ^ { k } = L _ { k } ^ { 2 }$, which we shall denote by $\mathscr { G } ^ { k }$. Its 'complexification' $\left( \mathscr { G } ^ { c } \right) ^ { k }$ is the (complex) Lie group of all automorphisms of $E$ of class $H ^ { k }$. Since automorphisms act on connections by affine transformations it follows from (14.5) that we can define the space of unitary connections $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ of class $H ^ { k - 1 }$ and that $\mathscr { G } ^ { k }$ acts smoothly on $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$. For the same reasons, when we view $\mathscr { A }$ as the space $\mathscr { C }$ of (almost) complex structures (or $\mathrm { d } ^ { \prime \prime }$-operators) we see that the complex Lie group $\left( \mathscr { G } ^ { c } \right) ^ { k }$ also acts smoothly on $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$.

For $k > 2$ the space $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ consists of continuous connections. However, the most natural space for our purposes is in fact $\mathscr { A } ^ { 1 }$, so the reader should remember that this includes discontinuous connections. A little more care will be necessary in various places but there is no fundamental difficulty. As an indication of this we shall establish the following regularity results.

Lemma 14.6. For $k \geqslant 2$ and any $A \in \mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ let $F : ( \mathscr { G } \mathfrak { c } ) ^ { k } \rightarrow \mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ be the map given by the action on $A$ i.e. $F ( g ) = g ( A )$. Then the differential $\mathrm { d } F$ at the identity is a Fredholm operator.

Proof. The differential $\mathrm { d } F$ at the identity is just the operator $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime }$ acting from $H ^ { k }$-sections of End $E$ to $H ^ { k - 1 }$-sections of $\Omega ^ { 0,1 }$ (End $E$ ). If we fix a standard $C ^ { \infty }$ connection $A _ { 0 }$ on $E$ then $A = A _ { 0 } + B$ and
\[
\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime } \phi = \mathrm { d } _ { 0 } ^ { \prime \prime } \phi + [ B , \phi ] .
\]
Since $B \in H ^ { k - 1 }$ and $\phi \in H ^ { k }$ the mapping $\phi \rightarrow [ B , \phi ]$ can by (14.5) be factored through the compact inclusion $H ^ { k } \rightarrow H ^ { k - \frac { 1 } { 2 } }$ and so is compact. Since, for the smooth connection $A _ { 0 }$ the operator $\mathrm { d } _ { 0 } ^ { \prime \prime }$ is elliptic of order 1 and so Fredholm, it follows that $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime }$ is also a Fredholm operator.

Applying the smooth group action it follows that $\mathrm { d } F$ is a Fredholm operator at all points of the orbit of $A$. The implicit function theorem for Banach manifolds then implies (for $k \geqslant 2$ )
\begin{itemize}
\item[(14.7)] for neighbourhoods $U$ of the identity in $( \mathscr { G } c ) ^ { k }$ and $V$ of $A$ in $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$, the image $U ( A )$ is a closed Banach submanifold of $V$ of finite codimension.
\end{itemize}

From this we shall deduce
Lemma 14.8. For $k \geqslant 2$, every $( \mathscr { G } c ) ^ { k }$-orbit in $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ contains a $C ^ { \infty }$-connection.
Proof. Let $N$ be a finite-dimensional subspace of $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ transversal to the orbit at $A$, i.e. $N$ is a complement to the image of $\mathrm { d } F$. Then (14.7) implies that for a suitably small neighbourhood $V$ of $A \in \mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ we have a continuous map $\pi : V \rightarrow N$ with $\pi ^ { - 1 } ( A ) = U ( A )$. Now for any $r + 1$ points $B _ { 1 } , \ldots , B _ { r + 1 } \in V$ (where $r = \operatorname { dim } N$ ) let
\[
f _ { B } : \sigma ^ { r } \rightarrow V
\]
be the affine linear map sending the vertices of $\sigma ^ { r }$ to the $B _ { i }$. Composing with $\pi$ we then get a continuous map
\[
\pi f _ { B } : \sigma ^ { r } \rightarrow N ,
\]
which depends continuously on the $B _ { i }$. Start now with any set of $B _ { i } \in N$ that span a maximaldimensional simplex having $A$ as barycentre. For this choice of $B , \pi f _ { B }$ restricts to give a map
\[
\partial \sigma ^ { r } \rightarrow N - A ,
\]
which generates $H _ { r - 1 } ( N - A ) \cong Z$. By continuity it follows that this will be true for $\pi f _ { C }$ with $\left| C _ { i } - B _ { i } \right| < \epsilon$. Hence $\pi f _ { C }$ must take the value $A$ on some point of $\sigma ^ { r }$, i.e. $f _ { C } \left( \sigma ^ { r } \right)$ intersects $U ( A )$. Finally since $\mathscr { A } = \mathscr { A } ^ { \infty }$ is dense in $\mathscr { A } ^ { k - 1 }$ we can find such $C _ { i } \in \mathscr { A }$ for any $\epsilon$. Then every point of the linear span of $C _ { 1 } , \ldots , C _ { r + 1 }$ is in $\mathscr { A }$ and so the intersection $f _ { C } \left( \sigma ^ { r } \right) \cap U ( A )$ is in $\mathscr { A }$, proving that the orbit of $A$ contains a $C ^ { \infty }$-connection.

Conversely we shall prove
Lemma 14.9. For $k \geqslant 2$ let $A , B \in \mathscr { A }$ and $g \in ( \mathscr { G } \mathrm { c } ) ^ { k }$ with $B = g ( A )$. Then $g \in \mathscr { G } \mathrm { c }$, i.e. $g$ is $C ^ { \infty }$.
Proof. The two connections $B , A$ differ by a $C ^ { \infty }$ 1-form $B - A$. The condition $B = g ( A )$ is more explicitly written
\[
g ^ { - 1 } \mathrm {~d} _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime } g = ( B - A ) ^ { \prime \prime } ,
\]
where $\omega ^ { \prime \prime }$ is the (0, 1)-part of the 1-form $\omega$. Hence
\[
\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime } g = g ( B - A ) ^ { \prime \prime } .
\]
Since $( B - A ) ^ { \prime \prime } \in C ^ { \infty }$ and $g \in H ^ { k }$ the product lies also in $H ^ { k }$ (by (14.5)). The standard regularity theorem for the smooth elliptic operator $\mathrm { d } _ { A } ^ { \prime \prime }$ then implies that $g \in H ^ { k + 1 }$. By iteration this proves that $g$ is $C ^ { \infty }$.

We have now established all the local regularity properties that we need concerning the action of the group $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$ on the space $\mathscr { A }$. In particular the orbit through any point $A$ has, as local transversal, the harmonic space $H ^ { 0,1 } ( \operatorname { End } E )$, which is isomorphic to the sheaf cohomology group $H ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { End } E )$. The structure of nearby orbits is then entirely determined by their intersection with this (or any other) transversal slice $N$. More precisely the union of all nearby orbits in $\mathscr { A } ^ { k }$ is $( \mathscr { G } \mathrm { c } ) ^ { k }$-equivariantly homeomorphic to the fibre bundle over the orbit of $A$ with fibre $N$ and group the stabilizer of $A$ (which is finite-dimensional and consists of the automorphisms of the holomorphic bundle $E ( A )$ defined by $A$ ).

In the next section we shall use standard algebro-geometric methods to establish the global properties of our stratification. For the present we note simply that the stratification of $\mathscr { A }$, which has been defined so far only for smooth connections, extends naturally to $\mathscr { A } ^ { k }$ for any $k \geqslant 1$ by our regularity results. The discussion in § 7 can then all be made rigorous in terms of Sobolev spaces and Banach Lie groups. Thus the groups $\operatorname { Aut } ( E ) , \operatorname { Aut } \left( E _ { \mu } \right)$ will be replaced by the Banach Lie groups $\operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } ( E ) , \operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } \left( E _ { \mu } \right)$ and the space $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ by $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu } ^ { k }$, which can be identified with the homogeneous space
\[
\operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } ( E ) / \operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } \left( E _ { \mu } \right) .
\]
Similarly replacing $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } , \mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ by $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 } , \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 }$ we have a continuous map
\[
\operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } ( E ) \times \mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 } \rightarrow \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 } .
\]
This map is constant on the orbits of $\operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } \left( E _ { \mu } \right)$ and so induces a map
\[
\mathscr { H } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 } \rightarrow \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 } ,
\]
where $\mathscr { H } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 }$ denotes the total space of the homogeneous fibre bundle over $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu } ^ { k }$ with fibre $\mathscr { B } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 }$. Our regularity results tell us that this map is continuous and bijective. To establish that it is actually a homeomorphism we need finally to prove that the map
\[
f _ { \mu } ^ { k } : \mathscr { C } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 } \rightarrow \mathscr { F } _ { \mu } ^ { k }
\]
refining (7.9) is continuous. In other words we have to show that the canonical filtration varies continuously along $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$ with a 'gain of one derivative'.

Since the group $\operatorname { Aut } ^ { k } ( E )$ acts continuously on both $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu } ^ { k - 1 }$ and $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu } ^ { k }$, and commutes with $f _ { \mu } ^ { k }$, it will be sufficient by (14.8) to prove continuity of $f _ { \mu } ^ { k }$ at $C ^ { \infty }$ points $A$ of $\mathscr { C } _ { \mu }$. Moreover by our regularity theorems it will then be sufficient to prove continuity in the harmonic space $H ^ { 0,1 } ( \operatorname { End } E )$ at $A$ (or any other smooth transversal $N$ ). On such a finite-dimensional space all the Sobolev norms are now equivalent and the problem can be reduced to one of algebraic geometry, which will be dealt with in the next section.

Once the continuity of $f _ { \mu } ^ { k }$ is proved it follows that our strata are locally closed submanifolds of finite codimension. Moreover the homotopy properties of the various function spaces are all independent of $k$ by standard approximation theorems (Palais 1965, th..13.14). This then justifies our heuristic arguments in §7.

Finally we note the continuity properties of the Yang-Mills functional.
(14.11) The curvature $F ( A )$ extends by continuity to a quadratic function $\mathscr { A } ^ { 1 } \rightarrow H ^ { 0 } = L ^ { 2 }$, so that the Yang-Mills functional $L$ gives a smooth function $\mathscr { A } ^ { 1 } \rightarrow R$.

The proof (given under more general conditions by Uhlenbeck (1982)) is a straightforward consequence of the multiplicative properties of Sobolev spaces. In fact, writing $A \in \mathscr { A } ^ { 1 }$ in the form $A = A _ { 0 } + B$ with $A _ { 0 }$ a fixed $C ^ { \infty }$ connection, we see that
\[
F ( A ) = F \left( A _ { 0 } \right) + \mathrm { d } _ { 0 } B + \frac { 1 } { 2 } [ B , B ] .
\]
Since $B \in H ^ { 1 }$ we have $\mathrm { d } _ { 0 } B \in H ^ { 0 }$ and $[ B , B ] \in H ^ { 0 }$ (using the inclusion $H ^ { 1 } \rightarrow L _ { 0 } ^ { 4 }$ from (14.1) and the multiplication (14.4)).

This result explains why $\mathscr { A } ^ { 1 }$ is the most natural Sobolev space for the Yang-Mills functional, although for our purposes any $\mathscr { A } ^ { k }$ with $k \geqslant 1$ would do equally well.

As we have seen in earlier sections the strata should be seen as the Morse strata of the Yang-Mills functional. A more careful analysis of the gradient flow or some alternative differentialgeometric argument might be able to show this directly and in particular to establish that $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu } ^ { k }$ is a locally closed submanifold of $\mathscr { A } ^ { k }$ for all $\mu$. We have not found an argument on these lines, which is why we have to resort in the next section to algebraic geometry.

\section*{15. The stratification in algebraic geometry}

In this section $M$ will denote a complete non-singular algebraic curve defined over a ground field $k$ of characteristic zero. As observed by Harder \& Narasimhan (1975) the definition of semistability and the canonical filtration of vector bundles over $M$ does not require $k$ to be algebraically closed. In fact the uniqueness of the canonical filtration over $k$ implies that it is already defined over $k$. Moreover if $E _ { k }$ is a vector bundle defined over $k , E _ { K }$ its extension to any larger (finitelygenerated) field $K$, then
\[
E _ { k } \text { is semi-stable } \Leftrightarrow E _ { K } \text { is semi-stable. }
\]

To see this we may first replace $k$ by its algebraic closure in $K$ so that $K$ is purely transcendental over $k$. Assume now that $E _ { K }$ is not semi-stable so that there exists a sub-vector bundle $F _ { K }$ with $\mu \left( F _ { K } \right) > \mu \left( E _ { K } \right)$ where $\mu$ denotes as usual the normalized Chern class. If $K = k \left( x _ { 1 } , \ldots , x _ { n } \right)$ the bundles $E _ { K } , F _ { K }$ can be represented by vector bundles $\mathscr { E } , \mathscr { F }$ over $M \times U$ where $U$ is some Zariski open set of $k ^ { n }$. Moreover $\mathscr { E }$ can be taken to be the pull-back of $E _ { k }$ under the projection $M \times U \rightarrow M$. Now restrict $\mathscr { E } , \mathscr { F }$ to any point of $U$ algebraic over $k$ and we find a sub-bundle $F _ { k }$ of $E _ { k }$ with $\mu \left( F _ { k } \right) > \mu \left( E _ { k } \right)$ so that $E _ { k }$ is not semi-stable. The opposite implication is trivial so that (15.1) is proved.

Now let $k$ be algebraically closed, $S$ an irreducible algebraic variety over $k$ and let $\mathscr { E }$ be a vector bundle over $M \times S$, which we interpret as an algebraic family $E _ { s }$ of vector bundles over $M$ parametrized by $s \in S$. A fairly elementary result proved by Narasimhan \& Seshadri (1965) is that the set of points $s$ for which $E _ { s }$ is semi-stable is a constructible sub-set of $S$. We recall that a constructible set is a finite disjoint union of locally closed subsets in the Zariski topology, and $X$ is locally closed if it is open in the closure $\bar { X }$. Constructibility is preserved under finite unions, intersections, complements, direct and inverse images. Since we shall need to refine this result of Narsimhan \& Seshadri we recall the essentials of the proof. First one shows that any indecomposable bundle $F$ of smaller rank such that
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $\mu ( F ) > \mu = \mu \left( E _ { s } \right)$,
\item[(ii)] $\operatorname { Hom } \left( F , E _ { s } \right) \neq 0$ for some $s \in S$
\end{itemize}
must belong to one of a finite number of irreducible families. Let $T$ be the parameter space of one of these families. Then the subset $Z \subset T \times S$ consisting of all points $( t , s )$ such that
\[
\operatorname { Hom } \left( F _ { t } , E _ { s } \right) \neq 0
\]
is a closed subset. Its projection onto $S$ is not necessarily closed but it is constructible. This shows that the set of $s \in S$ for which $E _ { s }$ is not semi-stable is constructible and so therefore is the complementary set.

We want to prove the following
Lemma 15.2. Let $K = k ( S )$ be the function field of $S , E _ { K }$ the bundle over $M$ defined over $K$ arising from $\mathscr { E }$. Assume $E _ { K }$ is semi-stable, then there exists an open set $U \subset S$ such that $E _ { s }$ is semi-stable for all $s \in U$.

Proof. Assume the conclusion false. Then for at least one of the parameter spaces $T$ occurring above the corresponding sub-set $Z \subset T \times S$ must project onto a dense set of $S$ (i.e. containing an open set). Replace $Z$ by an irreducible component with the same property and it follows that $K ^ { \prime } = k ( Z )$ is an extension of $K = k ( S )$. The definition of $Z$, together with the coherence of direct images, shows that we have a non-zero homomorphism
\[
F _ { K ^ { \prime } } \rightarrow E _ { K ^ { \prime } } .
\]
Since $\mu \left( F _ { K ^ { \prime } } \right) > \mu \left( E _ { K ^ { \prime } } \right)$ this means $E _ { K ^ { \prime } }$ is not semi-stable. By (15.1) this means $E _ { K }$ is not semi-stable and gives the required contradiction.

We return now to consider a general family $E _ { s }$ parametrized by $S$. Passing to the quotient field $K = k ( S )$ we consider the canonical filtration of $E _ { K }$. This filtration can be represented by a filtration for the family $\mathscr { E }$ restricted to some open set $U \subset S$. The associated quotient bundles being semi-stable over $K$ will, by (15.2), remain semi-stable over suitable open subsets of $U$. Hence there is an open set $V \subset S$ so that our filtration is canonical at all points of $V$. In particular the type of $E _ { s }$ is constant for all $s \in V$. Removing $V$ from $S$ we get a variety (possibly reducible) of smaller dimension. Applying induction therefore we have proved the following

Proposition 15.3. Let $E _ { s }$ be a family of bundles over $M$ parametrized by $S$, and stratify $S$ according to the type of $E _ { s }$. Then each stratum is a constructible set.

Note. This result is proved by Shatz (1977) in a different way. Our proof, using the approach of Narasimhan \& Seshadri (1965), is more in line with the rest of our paper.

When $k = C$, the field of complex numbers, we have shown in § 8 that, with respect to the partial ordering studied in §12, the subset $\bigcup _ { \lambda \geqslant \mu } \mathscr { A } _ { \lambda }$ is closed in $\mathscr { A }$. This implies in particular that in any algebraic family, as in (15.3), the corresponding set $\bigcup _ { \lambda \geqslant \mu } S _ { \lambda }$ is closed in the usual complex numbers topology. Since it is also constructible it follows (Narasimhan \& Seshadri 1965, lemma 12.2) that it is Zariski closed. Hence each stratum $S _ { \lambda }$ must be locally closed in the Zariski topology (cf. Shatz 1977). This is nearly but not quite enough to show that the $\mathscr { A } _ { \mu }$ themselves are locally closed. For this we need to examine further the continuity properties of the canonical filtration.

The proof of 15.3 shows that over a Zariski dense open set $V$ of each stratum $S _ { \mu }$ the canonical filtration varies algebraically. If we introduce the appropriate flag-bundle $F _ { \mu }$ over $S _ { \mu }$ this means we have a regular section of $F _ { \mu }$ over $V$ : in particular this section is continuous (for the $C$-topology). In fact continuity holds everywhere:

Proposition 15.4. Let $E _ { s }$ be a family of bundles over $M$ parametrized by an irreducible variety $S$ and assume all $E _ { s }$ are of the same type $\mu$. Then there is a continuous filtration of the bundle $\mathscr { E }$ over $M \times S$ that induces the canonical filtration on each $E _ { s }$.

Proof. As we have observed above there will be a Zariski open set $V \subset S$ with the required property. Also we can proceed by induction on the length of the filtration so we can restrict essentially to filtrations of length two. Such a filtration is determined by a section $\xi$ of the appropriate Grassmann bundle, and it will be sufficient to show that the Zariski closure of $\xi$ over $M \times V$ coincides everywhere with the canonical section (because $\bar { \xi } \rightarrow S$ is then proper and bijective, hence a homeomorphism). Since every point in the Zariski closure can be approached along a curve we can suppose that $\operatorname { dim } S = 1$. Moreover there is no essential change in replacing $S$ by its desingularization so we may suppose $S$ non-singular. Our section $\xi$ over $M \times V$ is then a surface and its Zariski closure intersects the Grassmann bundle $G _ { s }$ over $M \times \{ s \}$, for $s \in S - V$, in some algebraic curve $\bar { \xi } _ { s }$. We have to show that $\bar { \xi } _ { s }$ is just the canonical section $\xi _ { s }$. Consider the irreducible curves that make up $\bar { \xi } _ { s }$. We claim there is just one of these, say $C$, giving a section of $G _ { s }$ over $M$ and any others, say $D _ { j }$, must lie entirely in the fibres (over points of $M$ ). The reason is purely homological: since $\xi$ is a section generically the intersection number of $\bar { \xi } _ { s }$ with a fibre over $G _ { s } \rightarrow M$ must be one. Now we shall use the assumption that $E _ { s }$ is of constant type to deduce that there are no $D _ { j }$. To do this let $F _ { s }$ be the universal vector bundle over $G _ { s }$, i.e. the fibre of $F _ { s }$ at a point $\gamma \in G _ { s }$ is the vector space represented by $\gamma$. Hence the bundle $\xi _ { s } ^ { * } \left( F _ { s } \right)$ over $M$ is by definition the canonical sub-bundle of $E _ { s }$ and so has Chern class $k _ { 1 }$ say (independent of $s$ ). On the other hand it is well known (cf. §8) that on the Grassmannian itself the universal bundle has negative Chern class. Hence
\[
c _ { 1 } \left( F _ { s } \mid D _ { j } \right) < 0
\]
for any component $D _ { j }$ of $\bar { \xi }$ lying in the fibres. On the other hand the intersection number
\[
c _ { 1 } \left( F _ { s } \right) \cdot \bar { \xi } _ { s }
\]
must be independent of $s$ and hence is equal to $k _ { 1 }$. But since $\bar { \xi } _ { s } = C + \sum D _ { j }$ we have
\[
k _ { 1 } = c _ { 1 } \left( F _ { s } \right) \cdot C + \sum _ { j } c _ { 1 } \left( F _ { s } \right) \cdot D _ { j } \leqslant c _ { 1 } \left( F _ { s } \right) \cdot C
\]
and the inequality is strict unless the $D _ { j }$ do not occur. Now $C$ defines a section of $G _ { s } \rightarrow M$ and so a sub-bundle $F$ of $E _ { s }$ with Chern class
\[
c _ { 1 } \left( F _ { s } \right) \cdot C > k
\]
if the $D _ { j }$ occur. But the assumption about constancy of type of the $E _ { s }$ means that no such $F _ { s }$ can exist. Hence $\bar { \xi } _ { s } = C$ and the proof is complete.

Remark. The continuous filtration $\xi$ in 15.4 defines a continuous section $\xi ( z , s )$ of the flag bundle over $M \times S$. Now, for each $s \in S , \xi$ is holomorphic in $z$ and hence its $z$-derivatives can be estimated in terms of its sup norm. This shows that $\xi$ is actually continuous from $S$ to the space $\mathscr { F } _ { \mu }$ (the smooth filtrations of $E$ over $M$ of type $\mu$ ).

Proposition 15.4 shows that, for an algebraic family of bundles over $M$, the canonical stratification is continuous. To prove the continuity of the map (14.10) it remains now to show that we can always construct 'sufficiently large' algebraic families. More precisely we need to show that for any $A \in \mathscr { E }$ we can find a smooth transversal $N$ to the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-orbit through $A$ that represents (locally) an algebraic family. This means we have to prove the following lemma.

Lemma 15.5. Let $E _ { 0 }$ be an algebraic vector bundle over the algebraic curve $M$. Then there exists an algebraic family of bundles $E _ { s }$ parametrized by a non-singular variety $S$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $E _ { 0 } \cong E _ { s _ { 0 } }$ for some $s _ { 0 } \in S$,
\item[(ii)] the infinitesimal deformation map
\[
\phi : T _ { s _ { 0 } } ( X ) \rightarrow H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } E _ { 0 } \right)
\]
is an isomorphism.
\end{itemize}

Before giving the proof we make a few comments on (ii). Here $T _ { s _ { 0 } } ( S )$ denotes the tangent space to $S$ at $s _ { \mathbf { 0 } }$. The map $\phi$ is defined quite generally in such circumstances as follows. Consider the sheaf $\mathcal { O } ^ { \mathbf { 1 } } ( M ) = \mathcal { O } ( M \times S ) / m ^ { \mathbf { 2 } }$ where $m$ is the ideal sheaf of $M \times s _ { \mathbf { 0 } }$ in $M \times S$. We then have an exact sequence of sheaves
\[
0 \rightarrow \mathcal { O } ( M ) \otimes T ^ { * } \rightarrow \mathcal { O } ^ { 1 } ( M ) \rightarrow \mathcal { O } ( M ) \rightarrow 0 ,
\]
where $T = T _ { s _ { 0 } } ( S )$. For the bundle $\mathscr { E }$ on $M \times S$ representing the family $E _ { s }$ we have correspondingly an exact sequence
\[
0 \rightarrow \left( \operatorname { End } E _ { 0 } \right) \otimes T ^ { * } \rightarrow \mathscr { O } ^ { 1 } ( M ) \otimes ( \operatorname { End } \mathscr { E } ) \rightarrow \operatorname { End } E _ { 0 } \rightarrow 0 .
\]
From the cohomology of this sequence we obtain the coboundary
\[
\delta : H ^ { 0 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } E _ { 0 } \right) \rightarrow H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } E _ { 0 } \right) \otimes T ^ { * } .
\]
The image of the identity endomorphism gives therefore an element of
\[
\operatorname { Hom } \left( T , H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } E _ { 0 } \right) \right)
\]
and this is the infinitesimal deformation map $\phi$.
From the Riemann surface point of view this map can also be defined as follows. First we restrict to a small neighbourhood $U$ of $s _ { 0 }$ in $S$ over which the family $E _ { s }$ is a product family, so that we can identify all $E _ { s }$ with $E _ { s _ { 0 } } = E _ { 0 }$ differentiably. Next fix a hermitian metric on $E _ { 0 }$ so that we get a family of unitary connections on $E _ { 0 }$ parametrized by $U$. This gives a map
\[
\psi : U \rightarrow \mathscr { A }
\]
with $\psi \left( s _ { 0 } \right) = A$ representing the bundle $E _ { 0 }$. The differential of $\psi$ at $s _ { 0 }$ is then a map of $T$ into the tangent space to $\mathscr { A }$ at $A$. Projecting onto the normal to the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-orbit then gives the infinitesimal
map. Thus the condition (ii) precisely guarantees that $N = \psi ( U )$ will be a smooth transversal to the $\mathscr { G } ^ { \mathrm { c } }$-orbit of $A$. As we observed in § 14 continuity of the map (14.10) is equivalent to continuity of the canonical filtration along the corresponding stratum in $N$. But $N$ is (locally) diffeomorphic to $U$ and so the continuity follows from proposition 15.4 (and the subsequent remark).

We return now to give the proof of lemma 15.5. Observe first that it is sufficient to find an $S$ with $\phi$ surjective, because we can then always pick a submanifold of $S$ transversal to the kernel of $\phi$ to get an isomorphism. We now proceed by induction on the rank $n$ of $E _ { 0 }$. Let $c _ { 1 } \left( E _ { 0 } \right) = q$. Then we can always represent $E _ { 0 }$ as an extension of the form
\[
0 \rightarrow I _ { n - 1 } ( - m ) \rightarrow E _ { 0 } \rightarrow L _ { 0 } \rightarrow 0 ,
\]
where $I _ { n - 1 }$ is a trivial bundle of rank $n - 1$ and $m$ is a suitably large integer (depending on $E _ { 0 }$ ). For the proof see Atiyah (1957) where a stronger result is proved, namely that for indecomposable $E _ { 0 }$ the integer $m$ depends only on $n , q$ and the genus of $M$. Moreover we can assume $m$ chosen so that
\[
q + m n > 2 g - 2 ,
\]
which will imply that
\[
\left\{ \begin{array} { l } 
H ^ { 0 } \left( M , L ^ { * } \otimes I _ { n - 1 } ( - m ) \right) = 0 \\
H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L \otimes I _ { n - 1 } ( m ) \right) = 0
\end{array} \right.
\]
Now consider bundles $E$ given by extensions of the form
\[
0 \rightarrow F ( - m ) \rightarrow E \rightarrow L \rightarrow 0 ,
\]
where $F$ is a bundle of rank $n - 1$ and $c _ { 1 } ( F ) = 0$ while $L$ is a line-bundle with
\[
c _ { 1 } ( L ) = c _ { 1 } \left( L _ { 0 } \right) = q + m ( n - 1 ) .
\]
Applying our inductive hypothesis to the trivial bundle $I _ { n - 1 }$ we obtain a family $F _ { r }$ parametrized by $r \in R$ having properties (i) and (ii). We then take for our family $E _ { s }$ all extensions of the form (15.7) with $F = F _ { r }$ parametrized by $R$, and $L$ parametrized by the Jacobian $J$ of $M$. Now extensions of this type are classified by elements of
\[
H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L ^ { * } \otimes F ( - m ) \right) .
\]
By (15.6) the corresponding $H ^ { 0 }$ vanishes when $F = I _ { n - 1 }$ and hence for all $F _ { r }$ with $r \in R _ { 1 }$, some Zariski neighbourhood of $r _ { 0 }$ in $R$. Then $H ^ { 1 }$ will have constant dimension and so our parameter space $S$ is fibred over $R _ { 1 } \times J$ with fibre the vector space (15.8).

We must now investigate $H ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { End } E )$ for any $E$ in our family. Denote by $\operatorname { End } ^ { \prime } E$ the subspace of endomorphisms preserving the exact sequence (15.7), and by $\operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E$ the quotient:
\[
0 \rightarrow \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime } E \rightarrow \operatorname { End } E \rightarrow \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E \rightarrow 0 .
\]
Clearly $\operatorname { End } ^ { \prime \prime } E = \operatorname { Hom } ( F ( - m ) , L )$. By (15.6) $H ^ { 1 }$ of this vanishes when $F = I _ { n - 1 }$ and so it will vanish for all $F _ { r }$ with $r \in R _ { 2 } \subset R _ { 1 }$, some new Zariski neighbourhood of $r _ { 0 }$ in $R _ { 1 }$. This then implies, from (15.9), that
\[
H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime } E \right) \rightarrow H ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { End } E )
\]
is surjective.
On the other hand we have the exact sequence
\[
0 \rightarrow L ^ { * } \otimes F ( - m ) \rightarrow \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime } E \rightarrow \operatorname { End } F \oplus \mathcal { O } \rightarrow 0 ,
\]
which gives the exact cohomology sequence
\[
\rightarrow H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L ^ { * } \otimes F ( - m ) \right) \rightarrow H ^ { 1 } \left( M , \operatorname { End } ^ { \prime } E \right) \rightarrow H ^ { 1 } ( M , \operatorname { End } F ) \oplus H ^ { 1 } ( M , \mathcal { O } ) \rightarrow 0 .
\]
If we now compute the infinitesimal deformation map for our family $S$ at the point $s _ { 0 }$, and recall that $S$ is fibred over $R _ { 2 } \times J$ with fibre $H ^ { 1 } \left( M , L ^ { * } \otimes F ( - m ) \right)$ we see that the surjectivity for $S$ follows from that of $R _ { 2 }$ and $J$. For $R _ { 2 }$ this is our induction assumption and for $J$ it is of course classical. Together with the surjectivity of (15.10) this completes the proof of lemma 15.5.

